WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:08.540
- Hello, everybody.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:09.373
Welcome.

00:00:09.373 --> 00:00:10.907
Thank you for coming to

00:00:10.907 --> 00:00:14.160
the 61st annual Edward
H. Hume Memorial Lecture.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.410
We're really happy to have you here,

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:18.722
partly because we did not
do this lecture last year

00:00:18.722 --> 00:00:19.555
due to COVID,

00:00:19.555 --> 00:00:21.213
so this is a bit of a reopening for us

00:00:21.213 --> 00:00:22.860
and we're really especially
happy to see you.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.532
You can learn more about Dr. Hume

00:00:25.532 --> 00:00:27.570
in the back of your brochure here.

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:28.890
He was a physician,

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:30.190
he was a Yale College graduate.

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:33.143
He spent a good part
of his career in China,

00:00:33.143 --> 00:00:34.130
where he founded this Hsiang-Ya Hospital

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:35.760
and medical school,

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.160
and has a connection to
the Yale-China association.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.928
I think some of our Yale-China
association friends are

00:00:40.928 --> 00:00:42.150
here tonight.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:43.550
But yes, as I said,

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:45.640
we're very happy to have you.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:48.204
I know a few people are
still making their way in.

00:00:48.204 --> 00:00:49.520
Please note this lecture
is being recorded.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:50.670
So if you ask a question,

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.410
you may show up on the audio,

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:54.830
which will be posted
to our YouTube channel.

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:57.173
Afterwards, if you know
anybody who missed it,

00:00:57.173 --> 00:00:58.610
please let them know
that it's being recorded

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:00.117
and will be posted.

00:01:00.117 --> 00:01:02.962
And so, yeah, without further ado,

00:01:02.962 --> 00:01:05.297
I will turn it over to Arne Westad,

00:01:05.297 --> 00:01:06.890
the Elihu Professor of History,

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:09.097
who will introduce our guest speaker.

00:01:09.097 --> 00:01:09.930
Thank you.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:18.760
- Thank you, Richard.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.850
So it's a great pleasure
for me to introduce

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:25.010
my friend, Professor Rana Mitter,

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:26.730
who is going to be speaking to us today

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:29.621
for the 61st Hume lecture,

00:01:29.621 --> 00:01:32.517
with a title like "Kite
Flies Against The Wind:

00:01:32.517 --> 00:01:36.357
"Internationalism, Identity and Ideology

00:01:36.357 --> 00:01:38.797
"in the Shaping of Postwar China

00:01:38.797 --> 00:01:40.990
"and a Legacy for Today."

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:43.810
So Rana is professor
of history and politics

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:45.980
of Modern China at Oxford University.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:48.940
He's a fellow of St. Cross College.

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:53.420
He was inaugural director
of the Oxford China Center

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:57.220
from 2013 to 2020,

00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:59.480
when he made his escape from that one

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:01.758
in order to do more books, I'm sure.

00:02:01.758 --> 00:02:04.150
He has done quite a few already.

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:06.780
I'm not gonna mention
all of them here, Rana,

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:08.300
but I'm just gonna highlight

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:11.750
some of those that have been
particularly influential

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:14.223
in terms of Professor Mitter's work.

00:02:15.507 --> 00:02:16.932
"A Bitter Revolution:

00:02:16.932 --> 00:02:19.330
"China's Struggle with the Modern World,"

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:22.870
which is his survey of 20th century China,

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:25.920
and probably the best one
that is available now,

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.633
was published in 2004.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:31.410
His book, "Forgotten Ally,"

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:34.405
as it was published
under the title of here.

00:02:34.405 --> 00:02:37.122
"Forgotten Ally: China's World War II,"

00:02:37.122 --> 00:02:38.940
published in 2013,

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:42.140
is certainly the most
distinguished history

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:45.168
of China's World War II.

00:02:45.168 --> 00:02:46.001
"The Anti-Japanese War,"

00:02:46.001 --> 00:02:48.013
as most Chinese will know it as.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:51.408
And one that had a tremendous reception

00:02:51.408 --> 00:02:54.040
when it came out.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:58.347
And most recently, a book
entitled "China's Good War:

00:02:58.347 --> 00:03:01.700
"How World War II is
Shaping a New Nationalism,"

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:04.573
which came out now almost two years ago.

00:03:06.766 --> 00:03:09.084
Now, that last book, I think,

00:03:09.084 --> 00:03:13.930
sets some of the background
for the lecture today,

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:17.391
at least in terms of some
of the questions asked.

00:03:17.391 --> 00:03:19.090
And I must say when I read that book,

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:21.448
I saw it as a tremendous success

00:03:21.448 --> 00:03:24.705
because it was able to do
many things at the same time.

00:03:24.705 --> 00:03:29.470
It was able to present an updated history

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:33.190
of how many Chinese
view Anti-Japanese War,

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:35.346
we know as the Second World War,

00:03:35.346 --> 00:03:37.551
but it was also able to deal

00:03:37.551 --> 00:03:40.450
with memory and identification,

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:43.400
and project that forward to today

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:46.797
in terms of how the memory of that war

00:03:46.797 --> 00:03:50.867
and the identity is created
by that play and inference...

00:03:50.867 --> 00:03:53.343
Have a strong influence today.

00:03:54.686 --> 00:03:56.333
Our colleague here at Yale,

00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:58.600
Jay Winter,

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:02.596
wrote in a review of the book,

00:04:02.596 --> 00:04:07.387
that "Mitter shows movingly
what Chinese people sing about

00:04:07.387 --> 00:04:08.737
"and weep about

00:04:08.737 --> 00:04:10.027
"when they turn their minds to

00:04:10.027 --> 00:04:13.307
"the devastating contours
of the Second World War.

00:04:13.307 --> 00:04:15.927
"Equally at home in provincial museums,

00:04:15.927 --> 00:04:17.267
"internet chat rooms,

00:04:17.267 --> 00:04:19.127
"and China's foreign ministry,

00:04:19.127 --> 00:04:22.207
"Mitter is a sure guide to
this ongoing reassessment

00:04:22.207 --> 00:04:24.083
"of the war and postwar.

00:04:24.946 --> 00:04:27.357
"His brilliant account," Jay says,

00:04:27.357 --> 00:04:29.817
"shows how nation has replaced class

00:04:29.817 --> 00:04:32.557
"in the moral narrative
China has constructed

00:04:32.557 --> 00:04:35.057
"to frame its national project."

00:04:35.057 --> 00:04:37.710
And I think that's a
really good summing up

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:42.057
of the strengths and the
significance of the book.

00:04:42.057 --> 00:04:45.450
So Rana, in his work,
is, first and foremost,

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:48.497
I would argue, a student
of Chinese nationalism

00:04:48.497 --> 00:04:50.680
in its various forms,

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:53.300
starting out with his
early work on Manchuria,

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:55.810
and then going all the way up to his work

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:59.360
on Chinese politics in the 21st century.

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:01.120
And as many people, he will know,

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:02.680
some better than me,

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:05.450
dealing with concepts of
Chinese nationalism is

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:08.010
a really difficult topic,

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:11.123
and I'm sure we are going to
hear more about that today.

00:05:11.123 --> 00:05:13.440
Rana has really put his stamp on

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:15.460
the historical discussion,

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:16.780
and historiographical discussion,

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:19.210
but also the contemporary discussion,

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:22.270
about the format, the significance,

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:25.580
and the role of Chinese nationalism.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:27.400
And he's been able to do that

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:29.480
because he's not just an historian doing

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:31.030
fantastic work at Oxford,

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:35.020
Rana is also a leading
British public intellectual,

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:38.270
not just on issues relating to China,

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:39.630
but on issues of history

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:44.290
and contemporary international
affairs in general.

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:46.800
And the impact that he has had

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.220
on this has been tremendous.

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:51.660
Having taught in Britain
for a very long time,

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:54.120
and even after coming to this country,

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:57.210
I'm amazed to see how many
people you've influenced,

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:00.319
including a lot of people
who do not work on China,

00:06:00.319 --> 00:06:05.319
in terms of how they understand
the contemporary world.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.490
So we are really, very lucky

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:10.582
to have you working in this field.

00:06:10.582 --> 00:06:11.520
I picked up...

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:14.300
Just before you came here,

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:18.820
I was trying to put together
this presentation, Rana,

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:20.550
and I found a recent interview with you

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:24.979
where you outlined how you
got interested in China

00:06:24.979 --> 00:06:26.470
in the first place,

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:28.460
and I just wanted to quote

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:29.930
a bit of that in conclusion.

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:32.157
So you say in this interview,

00:06:32.157 --> 00:06:34.998
"In the late 1980s and the early 1990s,

00:06:34.998 --> 00:06:38.017
"China was a tremendously remote place

00:06:38.017 --> 00:06:40.927
"in terms of British perceptions.

00:06:40.927 --> 00:06:42.977
"India has always, for Imperial reasons,

00:06:42.977 --> 00:06:44.517
"been the Asian connection

00:06:44.517 --> 00:06:46.927
"that's been stronger in Britain.

00:06:46.927 --> 00:06:49.817
"So this was a combination
of a very challenging

00:06:49.817 --> 00:06:51.638
"but interesting language,"

00:06:51.638 --> 00:06:54.278
I can certainly subscribe to that,

00:06:54.278 --> 00:06:57.278
"but also a culture that perhaps had

00:06:57.278 --> 00:07:00.777
"as little to do with the
things I've grown up with

00:07:00.777 --> 00:07:03.019
"as could possibly be imagined.

00:07:03.019 --> 00:07:04.617
"And although, in later decades,

00:07:04.617 --> 00:07:06.637
"China has come much closer to us,

00:07:06.637 --> 00:07:09.077
"that was by no means obvious.

00:07:09.077 --> 00:07:11.227
"So it was a leap in the dark,

00:07:11.227 --> 00:07:16.000
"but not one that I regretted
at any point since."

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:18.071
Well, neither have we, Rana,

00:07:18.071 --> 00:07:19.517
who've followed you work

00:07:19.517 --> 00:07:22.950
and learned from your work
over now, quite a long career

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:24.860
in the study of China.

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:27.470
I think it is a most fortuitous choice

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:28.758
that you made back then

00:07:28.758 --> 00:07:31.939
for all of those of us who have benefited

00:07:31.939 --> 00:07:33.559
from your writing,

00:07:33.559 --> 00:07:36.990
from both your early books,

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:40.050
and the books that you
produced most recently.

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:42.270
It might not have been an
immediate choice at the time,

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:44.750
but it certainly was a good one.

00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:46.210
So with that,

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:49.799
let us all welcome Professor
Rana Mitter to Yale.

00:07:49.799 --> 00:07:52.966
(audience applauding)

00:08:05.320 --> 00:08:07.270
- Before I do anything
else in this COVID age,

00:08:07.270 --> 00:08:08.240
I should ask Richard,

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:10.600
is this bottle of water for my use?

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:12.690
Excellent, very good indeed.

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:15.410
Always best to check on these matters.

00:08:15.410 --> 00:08:16.890
Could I start by saying thank you

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:18.480
very much to everyone who has come

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:20.462
in person to this event?

00:08:20.462 --> 00:08:21.460
And I know these days that agreeing

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:23.240
to come in person to event has

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:25.450
its own frisson around it,

00:08:25.450 --> 00:08:28.020
so I'm very grateful
that you've all chosen

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:31.080
to join us this this afternoon.

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:33.320
And could I also start, if I may,

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:35.974
with a couple of thanks?

00:08:35.974 --> 00:08:38.590
I'm very grateful to the committee

00:08:38.590 --> 00:08:42.340
who chose me to give the Edward
H. Hume Memorial Lecture.

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:44.470
I know, having looked
at some of the history,

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:46.033
that not only was Edward Hume himself

00:08:46.033 --> 00:08:48.960
clearly one of the many
distinguished Yale graduates

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:50.260
who made their mark in

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:52.060
and connected to China,

00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:55.850
but also I felt suitably
humbled by the range

00:08:55.850 --> 00:08:57.270
of very distinguished scholars

00:08:57.270 --> 00:08:59.030
who have given this beforehand.

00:08:59.030 --> 00:09:01.273
So I am aware of the...

00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:04.890
The honor that has done to me

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:07.150
by inviting me here for that.

00:09:07.150 --> 00:09:09.320
I'd also like to thank
Richard Sosa, Stephanie Kim,

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:10.650
and all the others who have done

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:13.510
the rather complex arrangements
to actually bring someone

00:09:13.510 --> 00:09:15.410
from Britain to America,

00:09:15.410 --> 00:09:17.470
something which used to
be quite simple to arrange

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:20.450
but now has its own complexities.

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:22.870
I'd also like, if I may, to say, briefly,

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:25.130
a word to two scholars:

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:28.520
One is my very old friend,
Professor Arne Westad,

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:31.610
whose work, like, I think, frankly,

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:34.409
most of the field of modern
history have been learning

00:09:34.409 --> 00:09:36.690
from the last 25 to 30 years.

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:38.220
Not to remind you, Arne about how long

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:40.090
you've been educating us all for,

00:09:40.090 --> 00:09:41.370
but I think it is fair to say that

00:09:41.370 --> 00:09:45.110
in terms of everything from
the history of postwar China,

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:47.287
a subject on which today
I'm going to touch with,

00:09:47.287 --> 00:09:49.350
and I hope in some ways engage

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:52.040
with some of the agendas
that Arne has challenged

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:53.430
our field with,

00:09:53.430 --> 00:09:55.860
but also well beyond that in terms

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:57.257
of the global Cold War,

00:09:57.257 --> 00:09:59.477
and a whole variety of what we might call

00:09:59.477 --> 00:10:01.740
"The History of our Own Era,"

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:03.710
a field in which, Arne, I think is

00:10:03.710 --> 00:10:06.010
genuinely an unmatched historian.

00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:08.670
His departure from the
UK a few years ago was

00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:10.010
a great loss to us,

00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:13.100
but it was an immense
gain not only to Yale,

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:15.370
but also I think to the wider field

00:10:15.370 --> 00:10:18.310
on which he's now able to stride.

00:10:18.310 --> 00:10:19.810
I'd also like to, if I may,

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:22.210
give a brief word to a
scholar who is not here,

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:24.930
but I think is here in a wider sense,

00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:27.010
and that is of course
the late Jonathan Spence.

00:10:27.010 --> 00:10:29.610
I only met Jonathan
Spence a couple of times.

00:10:29.610 --> 00:10:31.230
One, I'm glad to say, was actually here

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:34.410
at Yale University when
he was kind enough to come

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:37.271
and listened to a talk that I gave,

00:10:37.271 --> 00:10:39.754
but I have told many times before,

00:10:39.754 --> 00:10:41.797
and will very briefly tell the story of,

00:10:41.797 --> 00:10:44.320
in that early phase that Arne mentioned

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:47.770
when I was thinking, as
a school boy in England,

00:10:47.770 --> 00:10:51.079
what subject I might wish
to study at university,

00:10:51.079 --> 00:10:54.313
one of the books that fell to my hand...

00:10:54.313 --> 00:10:55.670
In fact, the first book that I ever read

00:10:55.670 --> 00:10:58.487
in Chinese history was "The
Gate of Heavenly Peace:

00:10:58.487 --> 00:11:00.170
"The Chinese and Their Revolution,"

00:11:00.170 --> 00:11:03.260
the sweeping panoramic story
of what I now think of...

00:11:03.260 --> 00:11:04.872
I didn't know it then,

00:11:04.872 --> 00:11:06.760
but what I now think of
is the May 4th generation

00:11:06.760 --> 00:11:09.255
and the revolution that they made.

00:11:09.255 --> 00:11:12.140
And I still have that rather
battered Penguin copy,

00:11:12.140 --> 00:11:14.940
which I bought in a bookstore
in Brighton, England,

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:17.970
quite easily to hand in the
study that I have today,

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:18.870
I've realized, to my horror,

00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:21.110
probably something like
three-and-a-half decades

00:11:21.110 --> 00:11:22.970
after I bought it.

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:25.970
So although, of course,
Jonathan Spence is no longer

00:11:25.970 --> 00:11:27.650
with us here at Yale,

00:11:27.650 --> 00:11:30.310
he is, of course, someone
whose reputation will live

00:11:30.310 --> 00:11:32.077
for a very long time in the field,

00:11:32.077 --> 00:11:36.220
and I'm personally very glad
to have learned from him also.

00:11:36.220 --> 00:11:38.350
So a lot to live up to today

00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:42.207
and I will do my best, but forgive me if,

00:11:42.207 --> 00:11:44.980
following in the footsteps
of various giants,

00:11:44.980 --> 00:11:47.580
I may stumble now and then.

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:49.720
But I think we should start, if I may,

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:51.280
with some of the bigger events

00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:53.200
that have been shaping understanding

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:57.100
of Chinese history in the
last few weeks and months

00:11:57.100 --> 00:12:01.602
before we return to a
period slightly longer ago.

00:12:01.602 --> 00:12:02.975
And this is where I hope that

00:12:02.975 --> 00:12:05.525
the technology is going to
allow us to follow this.

00:12:08.539 --> 00:12:09.439
Yes, there we are.

00:12:13.250 --> 00:12:14.150
Particularly in this audience,

00:12:14.150 --> 00:12:16.750
you may have paid some attention to

00:12:16.750 --> 00:12:20.420
the promulgation of the resolution

00:12:20.420 --> 00:12:23.290
on certain questions
in the Party's history

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:24.850
that was passed through the Sixth plenum

00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:28.000
of the Chinese Communist Party in Beijing,

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:31.409
and officially announced
just a few months ago.

00:12:31.409 --> 00:12:33.133
In fact, in November of 2021.

00:12:34.173 --> 00:12:35.710
You will have seen, if so,

00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:38.620
that the resolution does certain things.

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:40.630
It seals the historical status,

00:12:40.630 --> 00:12:42.120
as one sees in the posters here,

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:45.070
of Xi Jinping as China's leader,

00:12:45.070 --> 00:12:46.530
placing him in a very direct

00:12:46.530 --> 00:12:49.220
and high profile trajectory

00:12:49.220 --> 00:12:52.370
from Mao Zedong through Deng Xiaoping,

00:12:52.370 --> 00:12:54.030
again, all of them portrayed

00:12:54.030 --> 00:12:58.194
as you see there in the
poster on the right hand side,

00:12:58.194 --> 00:13:01.020
and then through the other leaders,

00:13:01.020 --> 00:13:04.126
Jiang Zemin, Hu Jintao, onto Xi Jinping.

00:13:04.126 --> 00:13:08.210
Those two previous leaders
though, Mao and Deng,

00:13:08.210 --> 00:13:10.090
were the only ones associated with

00:13:10.090 --> 00:13:14.107
previous such resolutions
in the party's history.

00:13:14.107 --> 00:13:17.340
One was, of course, the more recent,

00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:19.340
Deng Xiaoping, back in 1981,

00:13:19.340 --> 00:13:21.512
just 40 or 41 years ago,

00:13:21.512 --> 00:13:24.020
whose resolution essentially was

00:13:24.020 --> 00:13:27.155
an admission of at least
partial responsibility

00:13:27.155 --> 00:13:30.284
for the Cultural Revolution.

00:13:30.284 --> 00:13:32.490
But the previous resolution,

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:34.845
all the way back in 1945,

00:13:34.845 --> 00:13:39.160
was promulgated just as the
war with Japan was coming

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:40.447
to an end,

00:13:40.447 --> 00:13:43.110
and it helped Mao Zedong to confirm

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:44.840
the leadership of his party,

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:46.640
which had been a work in process

00:13:46.640 --> 00:13:49.370
but very much consolidating by that time.

00:13:49.370 --> 00:13:50.780
And that, of course, was a resolution

00:13:50.780 --> 00:13:52.650
that also laid down the ground

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:57.240
even before the civil war that
would eventually bring Mao

00:13:57.240 --> 00:13:59.807
and his party to victory.

00:13:59.807 --> 00:14:01.460
And I want to turn back

00:14:01.460 --> 00:14:04.720
to that date in particular, to 1945,

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:07.743
and talk a little bit about how I think

00:14:07.743 --> 00:14:11.190
that that moment should be rediscovered

00:14:11.190 --> 00:14:12.620
in terms of understanding not only,

00:14:12.620 --> 00:14:14.420
I think, some of the significance of

00:14:15.307 --> 00:14:16.140
the history of that moment,

00:14:16.140 --> 00:14:19.527
but also where it links
to the present day.

00:14:19.527 --> 00:14:22.640
And I want to do that by putting forward

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:26.464
an argument that the
factors that underpinned

00:14:26.464 --> 00:14:28.780
the struggle for order

00:14:28.780 --> 00:14:32.290
and the means of achieving that order have

00:14:32.290 --> 00:14:34.620
important parallels for a story

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:36.650
that emerges at that time,

00:14:36.650 --> 00:14:38.666
but which we are still living with

00:14:38.666 --> 00:14:40.480
in a different form in the present day.

00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:44.340
And that is the story of
the rise of a global China,

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:47.884
and a China that thinks about itself

00:14:47.884 --> 00:14:50.670
and thinks about its place in the world

00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:53.230
in some particular ways.

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:56.550
And for reasons of
organization and convenience,

00:14:56.550 --> 00:14:58.510
but I hope ones that will make some sense,

00:14:58.510 --> 00:15:02.050
I've divided that into
three areas of examination,

00:15:02.050 --> 00:15:04.200
all of which conveniently
begin with the letter, "I":

00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:07.981
Internationalism, ideology, and identity.

00:15:07.981 --> 00:15:11.570
And the moment, the period, the era

00:15:11.570 --> 00:15:14.170
in which I want to place them is

00:15:14.170 --> 00:15:18.929
a period that I started to
call "The Chinese Postwar."

00:15:18.929 --> 00:15:21.040
This is, of course, a reference

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:22.970
to the years after World War II,

00:15:22.970 --> 00:15:25.720
but it's also attempt
to try and popularize

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:27.730
the use of the term "postwar,"

00:15:27.730 --> 00:15:29.550
in other words, post-Second World War,

00:15:29.550 --> 00:15:31.490
post Anti-Japanese War,

00:15:31.490 --> 00:15:32.900
in the Chinese context

00:15:32.900 --> 00:15:34.510
in a way that is entirely mainstream

00:15:34.510 --> 00:15:37.630
and uncontroversial in
the European context,

00:15:37.630 --> 00:15:39.160
as the late Tony Judt, for one,

00:15:39.160 --> 00:15:40.770
would've put forward,

00:15:40.770 --> 00:15:43.098
and, of course, in the
(speaking foreign language)

00:15:43.098 --> 00:15:44.268
in the Jap...

00:15:44.268 --> 00:15:45.101
Sorry, the Japan history field,

00:15:45.101 --> 00:15:46.550
where it's been a well known term.

00:15:46.550 --> 00:15:49.203
The Chinese equivalent,
(speaking foreign language),

00:15:49.203 --> 00:15:50.490
has, of course, existed for a long time

00:15:50.490 --> 00:15:52.280
but it doesn't have quite, I think,

00:15:52.280 --> 00:15:57.045
the capacity to encompass a range of ideas

00:15:57.045 --> 00:15:58.990
about a period

00:15:58.990 --> 00:16:02.420
as opposed to a literal
statement of the period itself

00:16:02.420 --> 00:16:04.818
in a way that there's other terms do.

00:16:04.818 --> 00:16:07.340
And so, I am putting it
forward at this point.

00:16:07.340 --> 00:16:08.530
It is, of course,

00:16:08.530 --> 00:16:12.370
a period about which
Arno Westad has written

00:16:12.370 --> 00:16:13.350
with immense distinction,

00:16:13.350 --> 00:16:15.350
the period of the Chinese Civil War,

00:16:15.350 --> 00:16:17.603
1946 to 1950.

00:16:18.580 --> 00:16:19.413
And if you didn't think

00:16:19.413 --> 00:16:20.970
the Chinese Civil War went on until 1950,

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:23.144
you should check out Hainan Island,

00:16:23.144 --> 00:16:26.128
where of course it did last
until quite late into that year.

00:16:26.128 --> 00:16:28.490
But I would say that also, of course,

00:16:28.490 --> 00:16:30.740
it is much more than the period

00:16:30.740 --> 00:16:31.900
of the Chinese Civil War,

00:16:31.900 --> 00:16:34.900
devastating and central though that was.

00:16:34.900 --> 00:16:36.580
It was also the time when China moved

00:16:36.580 --> 00:16:39.430
into the first phase of its engagement

00:16:39.430 --> 00:16:42.050
with the postwar international order.

00:16:42.050 --> 00:16:44.646
And I speak, of course,
today on a day when,

00:16:44.646 --> 00:16:47.170
largely to do with
events in Eastern Europe

00:16:47.170 --> 00:16:48.830
rather than East Asia,

00:16:48.830 --> 00:16:50.760
the nature of that postwar order,

00:16:50.760 --> 00:16:54.450
already somewhat fragile for good reasons,

00:16:54.450 --> 00:16:56.940
is becoming even more in question

00:16:58.910 --> 00:17:02.850
as the hours and the days go by.

00:17:02.850 --> 00:17:06.830
It's also, of course, a
moment when China sought

00:17:06.830 --> 00:17:08.770
a new definition of its own sovereignty

00:17:08.770 --> 00:17:11.800
as a founder member of the United Nations.

00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:13.780
And of course, a permanent member

00:17:13.780 --> 00:17:16.610
of the newly formed Security Council.

00:17:16.610 --> 00:17:19.210
It was also a time when
China became embroiled

00:17:19.210 --> 00:17:21.510
in some of the biggest global debates

00:17:21.510 --> 00:17:25.978
about the links between
economic and social development.

00:17:25.978 --> 00:17:29.030
It was also a time when
ideological concerns did,

00:17:29.030 --> 00:17:31.460
I think, come to the forefront.

00:17:31.460 --> 00:17:32.960
There were huge debates in China

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:35.010
in those postwar years

00:17:35.010 --> 00:17:38.225
about what democracy might actually mean

00:17:38.225 --> 00:17:40.181
in the Chinese context.

00:17:40.181 --> 00:17:43.940
And of course, huge debates
about what relationship, if any,

00:17:43.940 --> 00:17:45.520
that type of democracy had

00:17:45.520 --> 00:17:49.460
to the growing, and
ultimately inexorable forces,

00:17:49.460 --> 00:17:52.482
in China's deep countryside.

00:17:52.482 --> 00:17:53.724
In other words,

00:17:53.724 --> 00:17:56.610
it was a time when some
of the debates were

00:17:56.610 --> 00:17:59.680
remarkably similar to those of today,

00:17:59.680 --> 00:18:01.270
and in other important ways,

00:18:01.270 --> 00:18:03.550
remarkably different.

00:18:03.550 --> 00:18:06.520
The China of Xi Jinping,
I should be clear,

00:18:06.520 --> 00:18:09.150
is not, in any argument that I will make,

00:18:09.150 --> 00:18:13.700
the same has the China that
Mao Zedong took over in 1949.

00:18:14.830 --> 00:18:16.840
Today's China is global in reach,

00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:18.410
whereas that China was recovering

00:18:18.410 --> 00:18:22.130
from the aftermath of two huge wars:

00:18:22.130 --> 00:18:23.510
First with Japan and then, of course,

00:18:23.510 --> 00:18:25.580
between the Chinese themselves.

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:27.650
Today's China is, I think, indubitably

00:18:27.650 --> 00:18:29.082
a consumerist society,

00:18:29.082 --> 00:18:33.082
the earlier version was
very much about austerity.

00:18:33.082 --> 00:18:36.420
Today's China is an
innovator in technology,

00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:38.610
that earlier version was
desperate to find support,

00:18:38.610 --> 00:18:41.330
whether from the Soviets
or from the Americans,

00:18:41.330 --> 00:18:44.830
to create its own ecology of development

00:18:44.830 --> 00:18:46.683
that will become autonomous over time

00:18:46.683 --> 00:18:50.540
but could not start in that way.

00:18:50.540 --> 00:18:53.243
So the differences are clear,

00:18:53.243 --> 00:18:58.243
but the continuities do matter.

00:18:58.270 --> 00:19:00.550
To understand why that would be,

00:19:00.550 --> 00:19:01.870
you just have to look at the process

00:19:01.870 --> 00:19:03.270
that was going on to produce

00:19:03.270 --> 00:19:07.620
the resolution on the party's history.

00:19:07.620 --> 00:19:12.136
And the key word in that,

00:19:12.136 --> 00:19:13.750
perhaps unsurprisingly,

00:19:13.750 --> 00:19:15.280
is the word "history."

00:19:15.280 --> 00:19:16.970
In other words, the CCP,

00:19:16.970 --> 00:19:18.550
the Chinese Communist Party,

00:19:18.550 --> 00:19:20.873
cares deeply that its own version

00:19:20.873 --> 00:19:24.450
of its own story of origin is dominant

00:19:24.450 --> 00:19:27.049
without any of the
nasty bumps in the road,

00:19:27.049 --> 00:19:29.458
vicious intra-party massacres,

00:19:29.458 --> 00:19:31.753
the famine of the Great Leap Forward,

00:19:31.753 --> 00:19:33.490
the turmoil of the Culture Revolution,

00:19:33.490 --> 00:19:36.360
that in practice have been
part of the historical nexus

00:19:36.360 --> 00:19:39.660
of events that have brought it to power.

00:19:39.660 --> 00:19:40.520
And you can see that,

00:19:40.520 --> 00:19:41.900
I won't go through the whole thing,

00:19:41.900 --> 00:19:42.740
you'll be glad to know,

00:19:42.740 --> 00:19:44.290
but here are a few key quotes from

00:19:44.290 --> 00:19:46.730
the authorized English translation

00:19:46.730 --> 00:19:50.253
of the original text as issued.

00:19:51.380 --> 00:19:53.940
And I go into that detail because

00:19:53.940 --> 00:19:56.930
although we have a relatively
limited time today,

00:19:56.930 --> 00:19:59.350
I think it is fair to say
that that history is becoming

00:19:59.350 --> 00:20:02.090
of increasing interest
both in China itself

00:20:02.090 --> 00:20:03.510
and in the wider world.

00:20:03.510 --> 00:20:05.050
And actually, in some ways,

00:20:05.050 --> 00:20:07.678
the interest is a bifurcated one.

00:20:07.678 --> 00:20:10.130
One is a series of questions of interest

00:20:10.130 --> 00:20:12.900
within the historical profession,

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:14.680
and in today's comments,

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:17.010
when I get to some
detail in just a moment,

00:20:17.010 --> 00:20:19.500
I think it is fair to say
that those debates are ones

00:20:19.500 --> 00:20:22.240
that I think historians are
primarily concerned with,

00:20:22.240 --> 00:20:25.550
and which I think can help
us rethink, beyond China,

00:20:25.550 --> 00:20:27.659
this period of global history.

00:20:27.659 --> 00:20:30.330
But the other set of concerns is

00:20:30.330 --> 00:20:32.170
very much an engagement with the idea

00:20:32.170 --> 00:20:34.607
that shape China's contemporary politics,

00:20:34.607 --> 00:20:38.270
and I give you this short,
if somewhat dense, extract

00:20:38.270 --> 00:20:40.507
from the party resolution of 2021,

00:20:40.507 --> 00:20:43.320
simply to point out the
Chinese Communist Party

00:20:43.320 --> 00:20:44.960
in its present incarnation,

00:20:44.960 --> 00:20:46.610
however future-oriented,

00:20:46.610 --> 00:20:48.893
however keen it is on creating

00:20:48.893 --> 00:20:52.777
a forward-looking socioeconomic contract,

00:20:52.777 --> 00:20:55.210
is still immensely concerned with the way

00:20:55.210 --> 00:20:57.520
in which it's past is constructed

00:20:57.520 --> 00:20:58.830
in its own eyes

00:20:58.830 --> 00:21:02.477
and in the eyes of others.

00:21:02.477 --> 00:21:04.620
So to give you some illustration

00:21:04.620 --> 00:21:07.556
of how that continuity,
as well as change, works,

00:21:07.556 --> 00:21:09.360
I've drawn on historical
materials relating

00:21:09.360 --> 00:21:11.980
to various individuals with, I think,

00:21:11.980 --> 00:21:15.240
important insights into the geopolitical

00:21:15.240 --> 00:21:19.240
and ideological change during that period.

00:21:19.240 --> 00:21:21.560
I'm not gonna talk today very much

00:21:21.560 --> 00:21:24.270
about the very, what
you might kind of call,

00:21:24.270 --> 00:21:25.700
top tier of leaders,

00:21:25.700 --> 00:21:28.290
Mao Zedong, Chiang Kai-Shek, and others.

00:21:28.290 --> 00:21:29.440
I've written about them elsewhere,

00:21:29.440 --> 00:21:31.850
and there are many others
who have done wonderful work

00:21:31.850 --> 00:21:34.995
on what they were doing
during this period.

00:21:34.995 --> 00:21:37.740
Instead, I want to draw on some voices

00:21:37.740 --> 00:21:40.450
that I think are less well known,

00:21:40.450 --> 00:21:42.120
although I think they are quite central

00:21:42.120 --> 00:21:43.960
in terms of what they illustrate

00:21:43.960 --> 00:21:48.000
to the dynamics of that
postwar moment in China.

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:49.550
And I have to confess,

00:21:49.550 --> 00:21:52.710
I've chosen them also because
they allowed me to indulge

00:21:52.710 --> 00:21:54.180
what I personally have found

00:21:54.180 --> 00:21:55.760
one of the most rewarding parts

00:21:55.760 --> 00:21:58.460
of examination of historical development

00:21:58.460 --> 00:21:59.400
during this time,

00:21:59.400 --> 00:22:01.180
which is the diaries kept by

00:22:01.180 --> 00:22:03.280
very large numbers of figures,

00:22:03.280 --> 00:22:05.330
not only of world historical importance,

00:22:05.330 --> 00:22:08.310
but also quite local,
inward-looking importance.

00:22:08.310 --> 00:22:11.133
Day after day, I think
here Arne may agree,

00:22:11.133 --> 00:22:13.432
that historians of China give thanks for

00:22:13.432 --> 00:22:14.265
the Confucian tendencies

00:22:14.265 --> 00:22:16.580
that led very large numbers of Chinese,

00:22:16.580 --> 00:22:18.260
even into the mid 20th century,

00:22:18.260 --> 00:22:19.700
to keep very extensive diaries

00:22:19.700 --> 00:22:21.390
of their own thoughts and doings,

00:22:21.390 --> 00:22:24.257
and those have been very
valuable to all of us

00:22:24.257 --> 00:22:27.439
who want to reconstruct
that particular past.

00:22:27.439 --> 00:22:31.730
And I will here show a couple of pictures

00:22:31.730 --> 00:22:32.770
of two of the figures

00:22:32.770 --> 00:22:35.717
who I wanted to bring up,

00:22:35.717 --> 00:22:37.330
or will bring up.

00:22:37.330 --> 00:22:39.150
On the left hand side, a figure,

00:22:39.150 --> 00:22:42.056
I've said he was less famous
than some of the others

00:22:42.056 --> 00:22:42.950
but actually, at the time,

00:22:42.950 --> 00:22:44.860
pretty distinguished, Wang Shijie.

00:22:44.860 --> 00:22:46.670
During the time that I'm talking about,

00:22:46.670 --> 00:22:47.960
the foreign minister of China,

00:22:47.960 --> 00:22:49.890
but also a constitutional lawyer.

00:22:49.890 --> 00:22:53.720
One of that extraordinary
generation of young Chinese,

00:22:53.720 --> 00:22:54.860
almost all, but not entirely,

00:22:54.860 --> 00:22:57.590
but almost all men who were
sent to study overseas,

00:22:57.590 --> 00:22:59.230
mostly in the United States,

00:22:59.230 --> 00:23:00.840
some of them in Britain,

00:23:00.840 --> 00:23:04.270
and who became fluent not
just in English and Chinese,

00:23:04.270 --> 00:23:06.750
but actually fluent
into worldviews as well.

00:23:06.750 --> 00:23:08.670
And I think that that is something which,

00:23:08.670 --> 00:23:11.610
until recently, has been forgotten

00:23:11.610 --> 00:23:14.537
and perhaps underappreciated.

00:23:14.537 --> 00:23:17.850
On the right hand side,
drawing actually here

00:23:17.850 --> 00:23:19.290
on memoirs rather than diaries,

00:23:19.290 --> 00:23:22.190
but I think very, very
vivid ones in many ways,

00:23:22.190 --> 00:23:23.710
is Zeng Zhi.

00:23:23.710 --> 00:23:25.580
During the post-war years,

00:23:25.580 --> 00:23:27.776
she was a young communist activist.

00:23:27.776 --> 00:23:31.790
She joined the revolution
when she was just 15 years old

00:23:31.790 --> 00:23:33.620
in Hunan province.

00:23:33.620 --> 00:23:35.240
She spent most of the next 20 years

00:23:35.240 --> 00:23:36.936
essentially on the run.

00:23:36.936 --> 00:23:39.570
She was a powerful actor in her own right,

00:23:39.570 --> 00:23:41.870
but I should observe,
she also became married

00:23:41.870 --> 00:23:43.920
to a figure who became
very senior in the CCP,

00:23:43.920 --> 00:23:46.570
Politburo Standing
Committee Member, Tao Zhu,

00:23:46.570 --> 00:23:49.850
who came to a pretty harrowing end

00:23:49.850 --> 00:23:52.238
during the Cultural Revolution, in fact.

00:23:52.238 --> 00:23:55.820
And these figures I
think are amongst those

00:23:55.820 --> 00:23:57.590
who illustrate some of the wider points

00:23:57.590 --> 00:23:59.500
that I wanted to make.

00:23:59.500 --> 00:24:02.540
Wang Shijie himself, as he
observed in his diaries,

00:24:02.540 --> 00:24:04.880
the period of, first of all, hope

00:24:04.880 --> 00:24:06.700
and then despair of the last years

00:24:06.700 --> 00:24:10.059
of the Kuomintang
nationalists in government.

00:24:10.059 --> 00:24:12.870
As first of all, observing a period where

00:24:12.870 --> 00:24:16.960
all sorts of new possibilities
had opened up for China,

00:24:16.960 --> 00:24:20.420
but also one where the
country was deeply vulnerable.

00:24:20.420 --> 00:24:22.230
Bear in mind that in 1945,

00:24:22.230 --> 00:24:24.030
at the time of the Japanese surrender,

00:24:24.030 --> 00:24:26.901
China was simultaneously both stronger

00:24:26.901 --> 00:24:29.738
and weaker than it had been at any time

00:24:29.738 --> 00:24:31.370
in a century.

00:24:31.370 --> 00:24:33.330
Stronger, because it had the prestige

00:24:33.330 --> 00:24:35.640
of being a victorious Allied power

00:24:35.640 --> 00:24:38.020
alongside the British, the
Americans, the Soviets,

00:24:38.020 --> 00:24:39.940
and, of course, the French;

00:24:39.940 --> 00:24:40.970
and full sovereignty,

00:24:40.970 --> 00:24:43.856
essentially for the first time since 1843,

00:24:43.856 --> 00:24:44.689
because of wartime treaties

00:24:44.689 --> 00:24:48.670
that had been signed to
end various restrictions

00:24:48.670 --> 00:24:50.480
on its sovereignty.

00:24:50.480 --> 00:24:53.530
But at the same time,
weak beyond imagining

00:24:53.530 --> 00:24:54.610
because it was a country that,

00:24:54.610 --> 00:24:57.300
over the eight years of the
war against the Japanese,

00:24:57.300 --> 00:24:59.350
had been destroyed by bombing,

00:24:59.350 --> 00:25:03.463
refugee flight, and financial
and economic disaster.

00:25:04.313 --> 00:25:09.040
Zeng Zhi and other comrades of hers saw

00:25:09.040 --> 00:25:10.810
this postwar period as a time

00:25:10.810 --> 00:25:13.070
of ideological refashioning,

00:25:13.070 --> 00:25:16.180
when previous assumptions
about mentalities had

00:25:16.180 --> 00:25:18.200
to be rethought.

00:25:18.200 --> 00:25:22.220
It was not enough to rebuild
China's railways and roads,

00:25:22.220 --> 00:25:25.112
you had to refashion its people as well.

00:25:25.112 --> 00:25:28.820
And today, the reason I
bring this up is that,

00:25:28.820 --> 00:25:31.810
today, it's become quite commonplace

00:25:31.810 --> 00:25:33.940
to look at much of China's language,

00:25:33.940 --> 00:25:35.390
political language, official language,

00:25:35.390 --> 00:25:37.290
particularly at a time
when it really matters

00:25:37.290 --> 00:25:40.596
to know what the official
mind of China thinks and says.

00:25:40.596 --> 00:25:43.010
And from a Western point of view,

00:25:43.010 --> 00:25:43.980
quite often, that language,

00:25:43.980 --> 00:25:46.070
particularly when translated
directly into English,

00:25:46.070 --> 00:25:48.420
sounds either highly laden with jargon

00:25:48.420 --> 00:25:50.340
or very saccharine,

00:25:50.340 --> 00:25:52.110
or very, very confrontational,

00:25:52.110 --> 00:25:54.350
or indeed aggressive.

00:25:54.350 --> 00:25:57.500
And one of the things
that I think going back

00:25:57.500 --> 00:26:00.680
to some of that period
of birth and development

00:26:00.680 --> 00:26:02.910
of that sort of political language is

00:26:02.910 --> 00:26:04.490
a reminder that yes,

00:26:04.490 --> 00:26:06.870
it is highly constructed language,

00:26:06.870 --> 00:26:09.670
but that does not make
it inauthentic language.

00:26:09.670 --> 00:26:12.090
And I think the connection
between those two is

00:26:12.090 --> 00:26:14.300
very important in terms of understanding

00:26:14.300 --> 00:26:17.470
what people at that time
thought they were doing

00:26:17.470 --> 00:26:19.410
rather than what we think

00:26:19.410 --> 00:26:21.410
they may have been doing at the time,

00:26:21.410 --> 00:26:23.754
as I will hope to show.

00:26:23.754 --> 00:26:27.411
You early on in his tenure
as foreign minister,

00:26:27.411 --> 00:26:30.755
he served from 1945, August, to 1948,

00:26:30.755 --> 00:26:35.570
Wang Shijie became part of
the Chinese negotiation team

00:26:35.570 --> 00:26:38.270
with none other than the man
that Arne calls "the boss"

00:26:38.270 --> 00:26:41.080
in his books, Joseph Stalin.

00:26:41.080 --> 00:26:42.520
It was pretty much a baptism,

00:26:42.520 --> 00:26:44.450
if it was a baptism of sorts, of fire.

00:26:44.450 --> 00:26:46.740
Imagine your first
diplomatic negotiation being

00:26:46.740 --> 00:26:49.090
with Stalin the other end.

00:26:49.090 --> 00:26:50.890
Wang Shijie knew that it was going to be

00:26:50.890 --> 00:26:53.470
a pretty fierce sort of encounter,

00:26:53.470 --> 00:26:56.680
and as he got on the
plane to head to Moscow,

00:26:56.680 --> 00:26:57.670
he kept a little phrase that

00:26:57.670 --> 00:26:59.850
his daughter had written for him.

00:26:59.850 --> 00:27:03.813
And although the diaries
are in Chinese, a few...

00:27:03.813 --> 00:27:05.113
He was a fluent speaker of English

00:27:05.113 --> 00:27:06.293
and some phrases were in English.

00:27:06.293 --> 00:27:07.837
And this one, his
daughter had also written

00:27:07.837 --> 00:27:08.836
in English for him,

00:27:08.836 --> 00:27:11.527
and that was the phrase,
"Don't be afraid of opposition.

00:27:11.527 --> 00:27:14.837
"Remember, a kite flies against,

00:27:14.837 --> 00:27:17.113
"not with, the wind."

00:27:17.113 --> 00:27:19.410
And that was the source
of my title for today,

00:27:19.410 --> 00:27:20.660
but I chose it because I think it was

00:27:20.660 --> 00:27:22.750
a very appropriate metaphor for

00:27:22.750 --> 00:27:25.583
an immensely turbulent time.

00:27:27.840 --> 00:27:28.673
Let me...

00:27:29.716 --> 00:27:33.169
Before I start getting
into some of the detail

00:27:33.169 --> 00:27:35.730
of what Wang Shijie, Zeng Zhi,

00:27:35.730 --> 00:27:37.600
and others were saying at this time,

00:27:37.600 --> 00:27:38.890
I'm sure with this expert audience,

00:27:38.890 --> 00:27:40.728
we don't need much of this,

00:27:40.728 --> 00:27:42.750
but let me take about 90 seconds, I hope,

00:27:42.750 --> 00:27:45.110
just to give a very, very quick overview

00:27:45.110 --> 00:27:47.300
of some of the milestones, historically,

00:27:47.300 --> 00:27:50.400
that we see in China during this time.

00:27:50.400 --> 00:27:52.510
And as I've said, but will say again,

00:27:52.510 --> 00:27:53.480
if you want more details,

00:27:53.480 --> 00:27:55.297
you can't do better than to check out,

00:27:55.297 --> 00:27:57.960
"Cold War and Revolution"
and "Decisive Encounters,"

00:27:57.960 --> 00:28:02.740
two books by Arne, who has
pioneered this particular field.

00:28:02.740 --> 00:28:05.760
But during those five
very, very crowded years,

00:28:05.760 --> 00:28:07.410
it's worth noting that the war,

00:28:07.410 --> 00:28:10.040
which China had fought
with Japan since 1937,

00:28:10.040 --> 00:28:11.680
came to an end;

00:28:11.680 --> 00:28:16.680
Negotiations between the
nationalists, the Kuomintang,

00:28:17.670 --> 00:28:21.490
and the Soviets led to a
Sino-Soviet Treaty of Friendship

00:28:21.490 --> 00:28:22.840
in August, 1945

00:28:22.840 --> 00:28:24.820
that was essentially an
attempt, amongst other things,

00:28:24.820 --> 00:28:28.690
by the Chinese nationalists
to close off the Soviets,

00:28:28.690 --> 00:28:30.430
to give them certain concessions

00:28:30.430 --> 00:28:32.660
in return for them not attempting

00:28:32.660 --> 00:28:35.350
further support of their
Communist Chinese allies

00:28:35.350 --> 00:28:36.810
or further territorial demands,

00:28:36.810 --> 00:28:38.420
which include, of course at that time,

00:28:38.420 --> 00:28:40.310
incursions into Xinjiang

00:28:40.310 --> 00:28:42.633
as well as into Manchuria;

00:28:42.633 --> 00:28:46.232
China also during this becomes one of

00:28:46.232 --> 00:28:47.770
the permanent five members

00:28:47.770 --> 00:28:49.270
of the UN Security Council.

00:28:49.270 --> 00:28:51.210
China, of course, has been permanently

00:28:51.210 --> 00:28:52.950
on that council ever since,

00:28:52.950 --> 00:28:56.110
although, of course, for a
couple of decades as Taiwan,

00:28:56.110 --> 00:28:58.393
rather than as the mainland, until 1971;

00:29:00.970 --> 00:29:03.380
And also during this time,

00:29:03.380 --> 00:29:06.860
you see the increasingly
fraught coalition negotiations

00:29:06.860 --> 00:29:09.870
that begin in 1945-46.

00:29:09.870 --> 00:29:12.550
General Marshall, George
Marshall, spent most of 1946,

00:29:12.550 --> 00:29:14.650
ultimately vainly, trying to get

00:29:14.650 --> 00:29:16.110
a ceasefire and coalition government

00:29:16.110 --> 00:29:18.783
between the communists and nationalists,

00:29:18.783 --> 00:29:19.616
before that broke down;

00:29:19.616 --> 00:29:21.280
You then have 1946 to '49,

00:29:21.280 --> 00:29:24.750
a vicious civil war between the two sides.

00:29:24.750 --> 00:29:26.730
At first, there was a plausible argument

00:29:26.730 --> 00:29:29.000
that the Kuomintang,
that the nationalists,

00:29:29.000 --> 00:29:31.670
could somehow consolidate and destroy

00:29:31.670 --> 00:29:34.720
the CCP's political and military base,

00:29:34.720 --> 00:29:37.480
but a variety of factors turned the tide.

00:29:37.480 --> 00:29:40.800
Everything from improved
communist battle tactics

00:29:40.800 --> 00:29:42.470
under Lin Biao and others,

00:29:42.470 --> 00:29:44.240
to the destruction of the morale

00:29:44.240 --> 00:29:46.570
and economic base of the Kuomintang

00:29:46.570 --> 00:29:50.300
through poor military
choices and economic chaos.

00:29:50.300 --> 00:29:51.380
Eventually, the end of the story,

00:29:51.380 --> 00:29:53.115
of course, is well known.

00:29:53.115 --> 00:29:55.020
Chiang Kai-Shek flee to Taiwan

00:29:55.020 --> 00:29:58.380
and the communists conquer
the mainland by 1949,

00:29:58.380 --> 00:30:02.640
with Hainan Island
following the next year.

00:30:02.640 --> 00:30:06.831
So that is the main
narrative of the Civil War

00:30:06.831 --> 00:30:10.670
and the immensely important
backdrop to other events.

00:30:10.670 --> 00:30:13.580
But I want to put that,
if one can do that,

00:30:13.580 --> 00:30:15.870
slightly aside for a little bit,

00:30:15.870 --> 00:30:19.257
because it seems to me
that we sometimes act

00:30:19.257 --> 00:30:24.257
as if a phase in China's
foreign relations ends in 1949

00:30:25.360 --> 00:30:28.800
and starts again from zero.

00:30:28.800 --> 00:30:29.633
So from Mao's China,

00:30:29.633 --> 00:30:31.254
the turn to the Soviet Union,

00:30:31.254 --> 00:30:32.750
the split from the
United States made final

00:30:32.750 --> 00:30:34.140
by the Korean War,

00:30:34.140 --> 00:30:36.790
and the continued
presence of the ROC Taiwan

00:30:36.790 --> 00:30:40.140
in the United Nations until the 1970s.

00:30:40.140 --> 00:30:41.510
And in that version of history,

00:30:41.510 --> 00:30:42.410
I'm caricaturing slightly,

00:30:42.410 --> 00:30:44.490
but not all that much in some ways,

00:30:44.490 --> 00:30:47.042
1945 to '49 is a sort of stub,

00:30:47.042 --> 00:30:49.930
it's an afterthought,
it's unfinished business

00:30:49.930 --> 00:30:51.300
that finally gets wrapped up

00:30:51.300 --> 00:30:55.440
but isn't of huge
importance in its own right.

00:30:55.440 --> 00:30:56.450
And I'd wanna challenge that.

00:30:56.450 --> 00:30:58.370
I think although a lot did start again,

00:30:58.370 --> 00:31:00.590
evidently, in 1949,

00:31:00.590 --> 00:31:01.740
some important elements,

00:31:01.740 --> 00:31:04.550
including China's engagement
with the United Nations

00:31:04.550 --> 00:31:07.350
and the rivalry between
Taipei and Beijing,

00:31:07.350 --> 00:31:08.430
which emerged to make links

00:31:08.430 --> 00:31:10.790
with other non-Western states,

00:31:10.790 --> 00:31:15.790
remained highly relevant
across that 1949 boundary.

00:31:16.195 --> 00:31:18.278
And what makes Wang Shijie,

00:31:18.278 --> 00:31:19.400
the foreign minister of China,

00:31:19.400 --> 00:31:21.300
interesting in that time is

00:31:21.300 --> 00:31:24.210
the thread of things being taken up,

00:31:24.210 --> 00:31:25.650
or being set up,

00:31:25.650 --> 00:31:28.558
that would not be taken up by him in

00:31:28.558 --> 00:31:30.724
the immediate short term,

00:31:30.724 --> 00:31:33.530
but would later come back to become

00:31:33.530 --> 00:31:36.750
a very important part of
China's self-presentation

00:31:36.750 --> 00:31:40.620
in that later era of global engagement

00:31:40.620 --> 00:31:42.110
under the CCP.

00:31:42.110 --> 00:31:44.660
I'm talking about territorial claims,

00:31:44.660 --> 00:31:46.450
maritime and land based,

00:31:46.450 --> 00:31:48.210
and the wider projection of China

00:31:48.210 --> 00:31:53.070
as a power with regional
and even global ambitions.

00:31:53.070 --> 00:31:55.933
So in his first year
in office, 1945 to '46,

00:31:58.187 --> 00:32:00.200
Wang Shijie became part of

00:32:00.200 --> 00:32:03.470
at least three major negotiations.

00:32:03.470 --> 00:32:06.436
The first was the meeting
with Stalin in Moscow

00:32:06.436 --> 00:32:09.610
to bring about the
Sino-Soviet treaty of 1945;

00:32:09.610 --> 00:32:11.040
The second was his presence as part

00:32:11.040 --> 00:32:13.870
of that team negotiating
with American support

00:32:13.870 --> 00:32:15.423
for the coalition government

00:32:15.423 --> 00:32:17.390
between the Communists
and the Nationalists;

00:32:17.390 --> 00:32:21.130
But the third set of discussions related

00:32:21.130 --> 00:32:23.400
to areas that have been less covered

00:32:23.400 --> 00:32:25.839
and analyzed up to now,

00:32:25.839 --> 00:32:29.200
and they relate in particular
to Wang Shijie's attempts,

00:32:29.200 --> 00:32:31.900
at a fluid time in geopolitics,

00:32:31.900 --> 00:32:35.775
to stake a claim for
China as a global player

00:32:35.775 --> 00:32:39.080
in the postwar world.

00:32:39.080 --> 00:32:40.830
To steal the famous words

00:32:40.830 --> 00:32:43.510
of US Secretary of State, Dean Acheson,

00:32:43.510 --> 00:32:44.840
slightly (murmurs),

00:32:44.840 --> 00:32:47.107
he wasn't actually Secretary
of State at that point,

00:32:47.107 --> 00:32:49.684
"Not just to be present at the creation,

00:32:49.684 --> 00:32:53.617
"but also to take a stronger
role in doing the creating

00:32:53.617 --> 00:32:57.200
"of an order that would
stretch across continents."

00:32:57.200 --> 00:32:59.100
Bear in mind that Wang Shijie,

00:32:59.100 --> 00:33:01.980
like most Chinese officials and elites,

00:33:01.980 --> 00:33:04.039
whether Nationalists or Communist,

00:33:04.039 --> 00:33:07.160
absolutely believe that
China's titanic sacrifices,

00:33:07.160 --> 00:33:10.780
including the 10 million or more war dead

00:33:10.780 --> 00:33:12.170
through a variety of causes,

00:33:12.170 --> 00:33:13.919
military death, of course,

00:33:13.919 --> 00:33:15.742
but famine and disease as well,

00:33:15.742 --> 00:33:19.010
and of course, China's
refusal to surrender

00:33:19.010 --> 00:33:21.280
to Japan in 1938,

00:33:21.280 --> 00:33:24.920
their belief that these
events had changed the war

00:33:24.920 --> 00:33:29.310
and that the world now had
a debt of honor to China.

00:33:29.310 --> 00:33:33.990
And this led to a whole
variety of approaches,

00:33:35.020 --> 00:33:37.670
some at the more seemingly quixotic end

00:33:37.670 --> 00:33:40.570
of what might be geopolitically possible.

00:33:40.570 --> 00:33:43.939
In other words, Wang's pushing of the idea

00:33:43.939 --> 00:33:47.500
of China being a power that
might not just reshape Asia,

00:33:47.500 --> 00:33:49.360
and I'll say more about that in a moment,

00:33:49.360 --> 00:33:52.170
but also Europe as well.

00:33:52.170 --> 00:33:54.900
And Wang Shijie pushed
repeatedly for China

00:33:54.900 --> 00:33:56.750
to get a place at that table

00:33:56.750 --> 00:33:59.560
of European negotiation.

00:33:59.560 --> 00:34:00.450
That was, as I say,

00:34:00.450 --> 00:34:02.610
at the further end of his ambition

00:34:02.610 --> 00:34:03.880
at the time.

00:34:03.880 --> 00:34:05.420
More achievable in the short term was

00:34:05.420 --> 00:34:07.360
a desire to find a new mode of engagement

00:34:07.360 --> 00:34:09.380
with other countries in the emergent

00:34:09.380 --> 00:34:12.570
non-Western post-colonial world.

00:34:12.570 --> 00:34:13.940
China saw itself potentially

00:34:13.940 --> 00:34:15.630
as a mentor to many of them,

00:34:15.630 --> 00:34:17.124
but also, of course,

00:34:17.124 --> 00:34:18.700
in the case of countries
like India, in particular,

00:34:18.700 --> 00:34:21.500
a rival or an alternative leader.

00:34:21.500 --> 00:34:25.090
And Wang's daily negotiations
show many aspects of those,

00:34:25.090 --> 00:34:27.250
as we will see.

00:34:27.250 --> 00:34:28.800
The principle that

00:34:28.800 --> 00:34:31.570
if the west could still make decisions

00:34:31.570 --> 00:34:34.160
for Asia in 1945,

00:34:34.160 --> 00:34:37.570
as indeed it was doing
in Vietnam, in Malaya,

00:34:37.570 --> 00:34:38.650
in a whole variety...

00:34:38.650 --> 00:34:40.820
In Korea, in a whole variety of other...

00:34:40.820 --> 00:34:42.417
In Japan, indeed,

00:34:42.417 --> 00:34:44.640
of polities in the region,

00:34:44.640 --> 00:34:47.600
then likewise,

00:34:47.600 --> 00:34:49.720
Asia should be able to have a role

00:34:49.720 --> 00:34:51.650
in shaping the West.

00:34:51.650 --> 00:34:53.200
It might seem, as I say,

00:34:53.200 --> 00:34:55.610
an ambition beyond anything
that was realistic,

00:34:55.610 --> 00:34:58.200
but it's logic clearly appealed deeply

00:34:58.200 --> 00:35:02.600
to Wang Shijie's mind and, indeed, heart.

00:35:02.600 --> 00:35:04.020
And that led him, I will say,

00:35:04.020 --> 00:35:06.260
to some rather awkward conversations.

00:35:06.260 --> 00:35:09.030
One is illustrated, as it were, here

00:35:09.030 --> 00:35:11.064
in his engagement with the

00:35:11.064 --> 00:35:16.064
then Soviet foreign
minister, Vyacheslav Molotov.

00:35:16.418 --> 00:35:19.610
If you think that the current
foreign minister of Russia,

00:35:19.610 --> 00:35:22.010
Serge Lavrov, is something of a hard case,

00:35:22.010 --> 00:35:23.670
Molotov clearly set the mold.

00:35:23.670 --> 00:35:26.400
And I think, in fact, he's
related through family too.

00:35:26.400 --> 00:35:28.183
Marriage to the grandson, I think,

00:35:28.183 --> 00:35:29.394
or something that...

00:35:29.394 --> 00:35:31.260
Anyway, so there's a family
connection between the two,

00:35:31.260 --> 00:35:32.402
it must be said.

00:35:32.402 --> 00:35:34.423
And arriving in September 1945

00:35:34.423 --> 00:35:38.580
at the London Foreign
Ministers' Conference,

00:35:38.580 --> 00:35:41.340
one of Wang Shijie's first
priorities was to try

00:35:41.340 --> 00:35:45.201
and grab some of the big
foreign ministers of that time.

00:35:45.201 --> 00:35:46.983
Ernest Bevin of Britain,

00:35:46.983 --> 00:35:49.810
James Burns, the Secretary of
State of the United States,

00:35:49.810 --> 00:35:51.520
and Molotov.

00:35:51.520 --> 00:35:53.098
He didn't get very far with them,

00:35:53.098 --> 00:35:55.910
but nonetheless he
pressed his case strongly

00:35:55.910 --> 00:35:57.970
where he felt he could do so.

00:35:57.970 --> 00:35:59.170
Pushing the case, for instance,

00:35:59.170 --> 00:36:02.550
with Molotov that a trusteeship

00:36:02.550 --> 00:36:06.100
for the North African
colonies, such as Libya,

00:36:06.100 --> 00:36:08.300
which had been left behind by Italy,

00:36:08.300 --> 00:36:11.560
should be discussed in a much more open

00:36:11.560 --> 00:36:14.240
and full way than was
being planned at the time.

00:36:14.240 --> 00:36:16.130
And also, various plans
for the joint management,

00:36:16.130 --> 00:36:20.338
potentially, of the
governance of postwar Japan.

00:36:20.338 --> 00:36:22.980
Molotov was very keen on that idea.

00:36:22.980 --> 00:36:25.150
Wang Shijie cooled on it quite quickly,

00:36:25.150 --> 00:36:28.500
realizing that to do so would
almost certainly alienate

00:36:28.500 --> 00:36:29.600
the United States,

00:36:29.600 --> 00:36:33.160
but it was by no means a foolish request

00:36:33.160 --> 00:36:34.300
on the part of Molotov,

00:36:34.300 --> 00:36:36.540
since he could clearly
see the attractiveness

00:36:36.540 --> 00:36:37.800
of putting forward an option

00:36:37.800 --> 00:36:39.750
that would give the
Soviet more say in Japan,

00:36:39.750 --> 00:36:42.730
but would also attract
China by giving them a role

00:36:42.730 --> 00:36:45.340
as opposed to everything
essentially being decided

00:36:45.340 --> 00:36:48.230
by the United States.

00:36:48.230 --> 00:36:50.180
It's clear also from the negotiations

00:36:52.080 --> 00:36:55.640
as recorded, that Wang
Shijie put forward viewpoints

00:36:55.640 --> 00:36:58.160
and thoughts that drew
very much from his own

00:36:58.160 --> 00:37:02.380
and China's own experience
during the past two decades.

00:37:02.380 --> 00:37:07.380
I think the memories of Manchuria
being invaded in 1931 were

00:37:07.760 --> 00:37:09.620
very much in his mind when he argued

00:37:09.620 --> 00:37:11.650
against proposal by the French

00:37:11.650 --> 00:37:14.810
to separate off part of Northern Germany

00:37:14.810 --> 00:37:16.623
in the post war negotiations.

00:37:16.623 --> 00:37:18.207
Wang said in his diary,

00:37:18.207 --> 00:37:19.787
"I said that it wasn't right.

00:37:19.787 --> 00:37:22.347
"If the people who lived in
that region didn't desire

00:37:22.347 --> 00:37:24.677
"to separate from Germany, then...

00:37:24.677 --> 00:37:27.281
"If the people who lived in
that region didn't desire

00:37:27.281 --> 00:37:28.487
"to separate from Germany.

00:37:28.487 --> 00:37:31.864
"I talked about the
principle of nationalism,"

00:37:31.864 --> 00:37:33.677
(speaking foreign language)

00:37:33.677 --> 00:37:36.007
"which should not be neglected even

00:37:36.007 --> 00:37:38.170
"on the territory of your enemy."

00:37:38.170 --> 00:37:39.560
Now that's an interesting statement,

00:37:39.560 --> 00:37:41.590
particularly for the foreign
minister of a country

00:37:41.590 --> 00:37:43.730
that had suffered as much
as China had at that point,

00:37:43.730 --> 00:37:45.510
but also a sign of the, I think,

00:37:45.510 --> 00:37:48.418
genuinely quite forward-looking thinking

00:37:48.418 --> 00:37:49.595
that was going on,

00:37:49.595 --> 00:37:50.878
not about just what had happened,

00:37:50.878 --> 00:37:53.420
in terms of the immense
destruction visited on China,

00:37:53.420 --> 00:37:57.180
but what the world that was
coming was going to look like.

00:37:57.180 --> 00:37:59.943
He also went on very explicitly to say,

00:37:59.943 --> 00:38:02.665
his words, "China could not be excluded

00:38:02.665 --> 00:38:07.170
"from discussions of
the Balkans or Italy."

00:38:07.170 --> 00:38:08.667
I have to say Molotov's
reply was essentially,

00:38:08.667 --> 00:38:10.260
"Oh yes, China can be excluded"

00:38:10.260 --> 00:38:12.400
and indeed, pretty much
went on to do that.

00:38:12.400 --> 00:38:15.740
But again, the point is that
Wang was not simply refusing

00:38:15.740 --> 00:38:16.573
to put...

00:38:17.430 --> 00:38:18.370
Taking what he could get.

00:38:18.370 --> 00:38:21.220
He was very keen to make
sure that the was put forward

00:38:21.220 --> 00:38:22.610
and that China's argument

00:38:22.610 --> 00:38:25.510
that it should be a power
that at least had some say

00:38:25.510 --> 00:38:28.724
in global matters beyond
the Asian sphere was

00:38:28.724 --> 00:38:30.210
on the record.

00:38:30.210 --> 00:38:32.021
At that point, it has to be said,

00:38:32.021 --> 00:38:35.350
that Secretary Burns didn't
entirely resist the idea,

00:38:35.350 --> 00:38:36.790
although part of that was clearly to do

00:38:36.790 --> 00:38:39.521
with trying to make sure
that there was a balance

00:38:39.521 --> 00:38:40.354
between the various powers,

00:38:40.354 --> 00:38:42.070
all of whom wanted to get a certain role.

00:38:42.070 --> 00:38:43.760
And China, inevitably of course,

00:38:43.760 --> 00:38:47.680
was on the weaker end of
that particular spectrum.

00:38:47.680 --> 00:38:48.740
The following year,

00:38:48.740 --> 00:38:52.260
when the foreign ministers
met in Paris, in 1946,

00:38:52.260 --> 00:38:55.090
Wang Shijie was forced by stage,

00:38:55.090 --> 00:38:57.578
in a way that had been
less necessary in '45,

00:38:57.578 --> 00:39:00.270
to talk about and, to
some extent, make excuses

00:39:00.270 --> 00:39:02.770
for the continuing
failure of his government

00:39:02.770 --> 00:39:06.570
to resolve the civil war situation.

00:39:06.570 --> 00:39:08.197
But his earlier lobbying
did actually pay off,

00:39:08.197 --> 00:39:09.900
and China, at this conference,

00:39:09.900 --> 00:39:12.140
was given a certain
amount of time to speak

00:39:12.140 --> 00:39:14.804
about the European situation.

00:39:14.804 --> 00:39:17.330
One argued that the reparations

00:39:17.330 --> 00:39:20.090
for peace treaties being
drawn up mustn't be too harsh

00:39:20.090 --> 00:39:21.887
so as to avoid, as he put it,

00:39:21.887 --> 00:39:25.537
"Reactionary forces in the
defeated countries reviving

00:39:25.537 --> 00:39:29.570
"and democratic forces
being unable to rise."

00:39:29.570 --> 00:39:31.530
And again, in a moment,

00:39:31.530 --> 00:39:32.720
in a day, indeed,

00:39:32.720 --> 00:39:35.530
when the anger of powers
that consider that

00:39:35.530 --> 00:39:37.738
they have been placed outside

00:39:37.738 --> 00:39:39.420
the international order is being felt

00:39:39.420 --> 00:39:41.830
just across the Atlantic
in Europe at the moment,

00:39:41.830 --> 00:39:44.310
we can see, once again, that
these sorts of sentiments

00:39:44.310 --> 00:39:45.680
from 1945-46,

00:39:45.680 --> 00:39:47.760
while, at some level,
clearly very much tied

00:39:47.760 --> 00:39:49.380
to that historical moment,

00:39:49.380 --> 00:39:50.850
are by no means absent,

00:39:50.850 --> 00:39:55.723
in terms of that wider
principle of establishing order.

00:39:57.050 --> 00:39:58.440
Now, my point here is not...

00:39:58.440 --> 00:40:00.483
And there are various other things,

00:40:00.483 --> 00:40:01.678
of course, he talked about,

00:40:01.678 --> 00:40:02.904
including the question
of Italy's colonies,

00:40:02.904 --> 00:40:04.640
the Soviet refusal to withdraw from Iran,

00:40:04.640 --> 00:40:06.430
and a whole variety of other factors

00:40:06.430 --> 00:40:08.260
that emerged at that time.

00:40:08.260 --> 00:40:10.910
My point here is not that
China was a major actor

00:40:10.910 --> 00:40:13.140
in shaping the Balkan
or Italian settlements,

00:40:13.140 --> 00:40:14.580
clearly it wasn't.

00:40:14.580 --> 00:40:16.378
I think that would be a point

00:40:16.378 --> 00:40:18.070
of historical revisionism too far.

00:40:18.070 --> 00:40:19.560
What I think is important is

00:40:19.560 --> 00:40:21.550
the narrative of entitlement.

00:40:21.550 --> 00:40:23.460
Whether or not he got what he wanted,

00:40:23.460 --> 00:40:24.980
Wang Shijie, at that point,

00:40:24.980 --> 00:40:27.520
saw no reason why he
should not make a claim

00:40:27.520 --> 00:40:31.220
for China, as a wartime
Allied victorious power,

00:40:31.220 --> 00:40:36.220
to be part of the establishment
of that global order.

00:40:36.640 --> 00:40:38.530
And while the point of origin

00:40:38.530 --> 00:40:40.210
and the point of justification is

00:40:40.210 --> 00:40:42.200
in some ways different today,

00:40:42.200 --> 00:40:45.650
the fact that today a rather
different China makes claims,

00:40:45.650 --> 00:40:47.740
not just obviously in its own backyard,

00:40:47.740 --> 00:40:50.680
but in new areas, including the Arctic,

00:40:50.680 --> 00:40:54.340
does show that the idea of
China thinking of itself

00:40:54.340 --> 00:40:56.410
as a power that has global reach is

00:40:56.410 --> 00:40:58.740
something that has a much longer history

00:40:58.740 --> 00:41:00.330
that is sometimes assumed

00:41:00.330 --> 00:41:02.310
when the Belt and Road Initiative,

00:41:02.310 --> 00:41:03.800
starting seven or eight years ago,

00:41:03.800 --> 00:41:07.510
is thought of as a sort of year zero.

00:41:07.510 --> 00:41:10.250
In other words, China's
policymakers saw this

00:41:10.250 --> 00:41:11.690
as part of a trajectory,

00:41:11.690 --> 00:41:13.270
which, at that time,

00:41:13.270 --> 00:41:15.583
they were projecting into a world

00:41:15.583 --> 00:41:17.301
that would continue,
from their point of view,

00:41:17.301 --> 00:41:19.350
from the 1940s, 50s and beyond.

00:41:19.350 --> 00:41:20.630
They did not know at that stage,

00:41:20.630 --> 00:41:21.900
although they quickly found out,

00:41:21.900 --> 00:41:24.410
that their own particular
world would come to an end

00:41:24.410 --> 00:41:25.243
in 1949.

00:41:26.530 --> 00:41:28.170
But it didn't mean of course that

00:41:28.170 --> 00:41:30.570
their successors couldn't take advantage

00:41:30.570 --> 00:41:32.050
of what they had to say.

00:41:32.050 --> 00:41:33.630
And let me give an example

00:41:33.630 --> 00:41:36.050
of something closer to home from China

00:41:36.050 --> 00:41:37.850
and Wang Shijie's point of view

00:41:37.850 --> 00:41:39.680
that I think makes the
point quite clearly.

00:41:39.680 --> 00:41:43.320
Because while I put forward
Wang Shijie's attempts

00:41:43.320 --> 00:41:45.420
to try and influence
the European settlement

00:41:45.420 --> 00:41:50.420
as perhaps the furthest reach
that he might have tried

00:41:51.320 --> 00:41:53.610
in terms of making a claim

00:41:53.610 --> 00:41:55.130
for a new global China,

00:41:55.130 --> 00:41:57.610
the Asian sphere was not only an area

00:41:57.610 --> 00:41:59.540
where actually there was
much greater success,

00:41:59.540 --> 00:42:01.500
even in a relatively short period,

00:42:01.500 --> 00:42:03.680
but also one where the sponsoring powers,

00:42:03.680 --> 00:42:05.330
and in particular, the United States,

00:42:05.330 --> 00:42:09.510
were much happier to allow
a greater Chinese role.

00:42:09.510 --> 00:42:11.142
And this became quite clear

00:42:11.142 --> 00:42:14.080
in terms of the initiative that China took

00:42:14.080 --> 00:42:15.690
in some areas where it felt that

00:42:15.690 --> 00:42:18.323
it had newly earned rights.

00:42:18.323 --> 00:42:21.653
So let's take Wang Shijie,
looking a little closer to home,

00:42:22.502 --> 00:42:24.960
on the Xisha, the
Paracel Islands, in 1947.

00:42:24.960 --> 00:42:26.800
Now, I bring this
particular subject up again

00:42:26.800 --> 00:42:28.600
because, again, in this audience,

00:42:28.600 --> 00:42:30.341
I think it's fair to say,

00:42:30.341 --> 00:42:31.220
that you'll be aware that the problem

00:42:31.220 --> 00:42:34.140
of Chinese claims on
the South China Sea is

00:42:34.140 --> 00:42:37.200
by no means of only historical interest.

00:42:37.200 --> 00:42:39.540
I will not say anything more about rights

00:42:39.540 --> 00:42:41.460
or wrongs or yeses or nos

00:42:41.460 --> 00:42:43.750
about any present day claims,

00:42:43.750 --> 00:42:45.950
but I will read, if I may,

00:42:45.950 --> 00:42:48.250
a short extract from his diary,

00:42:48.250 --> 00:42:51.160
the 18th of January, 1947,

00:42:51.160 --> 00:42:53.330
which says something about the way

00:42:53.330 --> 00:42:58.180
in which Wang Shijie was
looking to put forward

00:42:58.180 --> 00:43:02.110
a new narrative of China's rights in Asia.

00:43:02.110 --> 00:43:05.860
In this case, pitched as
an anti-colonial move,

00:43:05.860 --> 00:43:09.030
because the power that was, at that stage,

00:43:09.030 --> 00:43:11.060
in the Chinese sites,

00:43:11.060 --> 00:43:13.650
when it came to reposession
of the islands in this case,

00:43:13.650 --> 00:43:15.330
was Colonial France,

00:43:15.330 --> 00:43:18.060
which wanted to reclaim
these particular islands

00:43:18.060 --> 00:43:21.280
essentially as part of
their Vietnamese colony.

00:43:21.280 --> 00:43:23.337
And Wang Shijie wrote in his diary,

00:43:23.337 --> 00:43:27.667
"We've always claimed
the Xisha archipelago

00:43:27.667 --> 00:43:28.740
"as our territory,"

00:43:28.740 --> 00:43:31.239
so there's grist to the
(indistinct) of today,

00:43:31.239 --> 00:43:33.850
as PRC making historical
claims on on the seas.

00:43:33.850 --> 00:43:35.127
But Wang Shijie continues,

00:43:35.127 --> 00:43:36.957
"But we've never yet set up officials

00:43:36.957 --> 00:43:39.610
"or sent workers to stay
there on a regular basis."

00:43:39.610 --> 00:43:41.597
And again, in the
present day (indistinct),

00:43:41.597 --> 00:43:43.128
there's lots of debates about whether

00:43:43.128 --> 00:43:43.961
or not these islands have been inhabited

00:43:44.835 --> 00:43:46.047
and having people actually live on them.

00:43:46.047 --> 00:43:48.367
"The French government
recognizes it," he says,

00:43:48.367 --> 00:43:50.477
"as Vietnamese territory.

00:43:50.477 --> 00:43:51.567
"After Japan's surrender,

00:43:51.567 --> 00:43:53.117
"we sent a small number of troops

00:43:53.117 --> 00:43:55.551
"to the important island at the heart

00:43:55.551 --> 00:43:56.776
"of the archipelago,"

00:43:56.776 --> 00:43:57.931
it's called Woody Island,

00:43:57.931 --> 00:43:59.368
"to occupy it.

00:43:59.368 --> 00:44:01.350
"Recently, the French
have sent a military ship

00:44:01.350 --> 00:44:02.937
"to the island to scare
our troops resident there.

00:44:02.937 --> 00:44:05.315
"But after my intervention, they withdrew.

00:44:05.315 --> 00:44:09.872
"This is going to be a dispute
between China and France."

00:44:09.872 --> 00:44:12.740
Well, I could say that
last sentence probably has

00:44:12.740 --> 00:44:16.288
not come out as being the
major historical element

00:44:16.288 --> 00:44:19.020
that was ultimately of importance,

00:44:19.020 --> 00:44:21.520
but all the rest of that
I think does indicate

00:44:21.520 --> 00:44:24.120
the way in which the claims

00:44:24.120 --> 00:44:27.240
and the desire to reorder the Asian sphere

00:44:27.240 --> 00:44:29.190
that was in China's influence was

00:44:29.190 --> 00:44:31.560
very much at the heart
of the Kuomintang project

00:44:31.560 --> 00:44:33.223
in the immediate postwar,

00:44:34.293 --> 00:44:36.993
well before the communist victory in 1949.

00:44:39.128 --> 00:44:40.952
And that happened...

00:44:40.952 --> 00:44:42.856
Again, it doesn't happen out of context.

00:44:42.856 --> 00:44:44.650
It happens during months and years

00:44:44.650 --> 00:44:48.020
when a whole variety of
different visitors come

00:44:48.020 --> 00:44:49.970
to Nanjing, the Chinese capital,

00:44:49.970 --> 00:44:54.110
to negotiate eight for
a new order in Asia.

00:44:54.110 --> 00:44:58.330
In 1946, the former Thai prime
minister, Pridi Banomyong,

00:44:58.330 --> 00:45:00.680
visits after the treaty with Thailand.

00:45:00.680 --> 00:45:04.762
As Wang said, "the first
time an important key figure

00:45:04.762 --> 00:45:06.370
"from Thailand has visited
China in this way."

00:45:06.370 --> 00:45:07.960
April 1947 was quite busy.

00:45:07.960 --> 00:45:10.580
Syngman Rhee came in from Korea.

00:45:10.580 --> 00:45:13.750
As Wang said, "His thinking
is a bit conservative,"

00:45:13.750 --> 00:45:15.110
which I think is a bit
of an understatement,

00:45:15.110 --> 00:45:16.687
but it's a fair analysis,

00:45:16.687 --> 00:45:19.720
"but his spirit of
struggle is very strong."

00:45:19.720 --> 00:45:22.550
And he was rather a fan of Rhee.

00:45:22.550 --> 00:45:24.160
And then, the Philippines,

00:45:24.160 --> 00:45:26.690
despite the disputes over South China Sea,

00:45:26.690 --> 00:45:27.960
more broadly speaking,

00:45:27.960 --> 00:45:30.470
in this case, a treaty
of friendship is set up

00:45:30.470 --> 00:45:35.470
between the Chinese and
the Philippines government

00:45:35.780 --> 00:45:38.990
just after the Philippines
gain independence.

00:45:38.990 --> 00:45:42.580
In other words, much more of
the configuration of order,

00:45:42.580 --> 00:45:47.040
it's being done by Asians with
other Asians at this point

00:45:47.040 --> 00:45:48.610
than has perhaps been appreciated.

00:45:48.610 --> 00:45:51.740
Not to the exclusion of
the superpower conflict

00:45:51.740 --> 00:45:53.270
that emerge at that time,

00:45:53.270 --> 00:45:57.520
and not to the exclusion of
the horrific domestic turmoil

00:45:57.520 --> 00:45:59.460
that is tearing China apart,

00:45:59.460 --> 00:46:00.960
but at the same time,

00:46:00.960 --> 00:46:03.770
and is the confluence of
these particular forces

00:46:03.770 --> 00:46:06.320
that I think deserves further examination

00:46:06.320 --> 00:46:09.190
in terms of their simultaneous actions.

00:46:12.350 --> 00:46:16.020
So let me move on from
that point, if I may,

00:46:16.020 --> 00:46:19.060
for the last section of what I want to do,

00:46:19.060 --> 00:46:23.210
to talk about a rather
different area of engagement.

00:46:23.210 --> 00:46:28.210
I have been ranging, in the
last quarter of an hour or so,

00:46:28.300 --> 00:46:30.530
in terms of this attempt to try

00:46:30.530 --> 00:46:32.610
and rewrite a little bit,

00:46:32.610 --> 00:46:34.271
the geopolitical project of China

00:46:34.271 --> 00:46:37.191
in this immediate postwar era.

00:46:37.191 --> 00:46:40.012
But I now want to sort of almost zoom

00:46:40.012 --> 00:46:41.574
in the other direction

00:46:41.574 --> 00:46:43.210
and look very much inside hearts and minds

00:46:43.210 --> 00:46:44.740
to understand what was going on

00:46:44.740 --> 00:46:46.960
in terms of changing in political thinking

00:46:46.960 --> 00:46:47.970
at this particular sense,

00:46:47.970 --> 00:46:49.610
and that's where I bring
together what I've called

00:46:49.610 --> 00:46:51.332
the other two I's:

00:46:51.332 --> 00:46:54.570
The ideology and the
identity of what going on.

00:46:54.570 --> 00:46:56.620
And again, not that everything

00:46:56.620 --> 00:46:58.810
in the past should reflect the present,

00:46:58.810 --> 00:47:02.018
but at a time when huge
amounts of effort is spent

00:47:02.018 --> 00:47:03.000
on reading Chinese documents,

00:47:03.000 --> 00:47:04.360
Chinese social media,

00:47:04.360 --> 00:47:06.540
I should say, actually CSIS
in Washington, DC, I believe,

00:47:06.540 --> 00:47:08.980
is just about to start a
major new online project

00:47:08.980 --> 00:47:11.133
in terms of analysis of
key Chinese documents,

00:47:11.133 --> 00:47:13.520
which has actually a very 1960s air to it,

00:47:13.520 --> 00:47:15.420
for those of us know where the feel the...

00:47:15.420 --> 00:47:16.253
Emerged from,

00:47:17.332 --> 00:47:18.504
although I personally wasn't around,

00:47:18.504 --> 00:47:20.290
I have to say, during
that particular moment.

00:47:20.290 --> 00:47:22.553
But that idea, in other words,

00:47:22.553 --> 00:47:23.730
that you can read an awful lot into

00:47:23.730 --> 00:47:26.010
how people construct language,

00:47:26.010 --> 00:47:29.140
particularly Marxist phrases and language,

00:47:29.140 --> 00:47:32.290
is I think one that has a much longer

00:47:32.290 --> 00:47:35.214
and very interesting history.

00:47:35.214 --> 00:47:37.570
In other words, we know a
great deal about ideology

00:47:37.570 --> 00:47:39.509
in terms of what was taught

00:47:39.509 --> 00:47:41.660
to converts to a particular
ideological cause.

00:47:41.660 --> 00:47:44.270
But sometimes, I think the missing link is

00:47:44.270 --> 00:47:47.260
to see how detailed the socialization was

00:47:47.260 --> 00:47:49.607
of that belief interlinks
to objective reality.

00:47:49.607 --> 00:47:51.960
I realize I'm sounding quite Marxist here,

00:47:51.960 --> 00:47:53.770
but what I meant was that

00:47:53.770 --> 00:47:55.590
these linguistic changes

00:47:55.590 --> 00:47:58.900
and ways of thinking do
not happen in a vacuum.

00:47:58.900 --> 00:48:00.390
I mean, how could they happen in vacuum?

00:48:00.390 --> 00:48:03.880
They happen in the context
of historical events

00:48:03.880 --> 00:48:06.360
and people use political
terms and language,

00:48:06.360 --> 00:48:09.180
constructed language that
comes from elsewhere,

00:48:09.180 --> 00:48:12.173
shape their own lived,
subjective experience.

00:48:12.173 --> 00:48:15.740
And terms like "lived
subjecting experience,"

00:48:15.740 --> 00:48:18.580
again, jargon for academics.

00:48:18.580 --> 00:48:20.650
We are talking here about
extraordinary people.

00:48:20.650 --> 00:48:23.470
Let's go back Zeng Zhi, who I
showed you at the beginning.

00:48:23.470 --> 00:48:27.130
So, as I said, at the age of 15, in 1926,

00:48:27.130 --> 00:48:29.487
she went on to a turbul...

00:48:29.487 --> 00:48:31.970
She joined a turbulent
life that went on and on

00:48:31.970 --> 00:48:35.210
in underground CCP politics.

00:48:35.210 --> 00:48:36.043
In her own words,

00:48:36.043 --> 00:48:38.687
she was "a career revolutionary."

00:48:38.687 --> 00:48:41.890
She killed her first landlord with a spear

00:48:41.890 --> 00:48:44.731
through her stomach in 1927.

00:48:44.731 --> 00:48:47.087
That's an interesting part of
her memoirs, I can tell you.

00:48:47.087 --> 00:48:50.750
And she sat within a grouping,

00:48:50.750 --> 00:48:52.590
a generation of people of different ages

00:48:52.590 --> 00:48:53.653
and different stages,

00:48:54.893 --> 00:48:57.390
all of whom became part of this new

00:48:57.390 --> 00:48:59.810
and transformative political project.

00:48:59.810 --> 00:49:03.432
A couple more here may
be known to you possibly,

00:49:03.432 --> 00:49:05.630
but in case not,

00:49:05.630 --> 00:49:07.954
on the left hand side, Yang Siyi ,

00:49:07.954 --> 00:49:11.594
a cadre at this time, in the
post war years, in his 40s,

00:49:11.594 --> 00:49:13.710
mostly based in Jinjiang,
in East Central China.

00:49:13.710 --> 00:49:15.670
Mid-level officer, not super high,

00:49:15.670 --> 00:49:18.700
but not a junior guy either.

00:49:18.700 --> 00:49:22.463
And on the right hand
side there, Liu Yanjin,

00:49:23.450 --> 00:49:26.910
the youngest of any of these people.

00:49:26.910 --> 00:49:28.200
Born in the 1925,

00:49:28.200 --> 00:49:33.194
just in her early 20s as the
1949 revolution approached.

00:49:33.194 --> 00:49:38.194
Let's have a look at some of
the diary entries of Yang Siyi,

00:49:40.340 --> 00:49:42.570
who, again, does historians a huge favor

00:49:42.570 --> 00:49:44.953
by writing not quite day to day,

00:49:45.913 --> 00:49:47.295
but certainly every few days

00:49:47.295 --> 00:49:49.190
as he is engaged in the campaign

00:49:49.190 --> 00:49:53.140
that ultimately becomes part
of the victorious campaign

00:49:53.140 --> 00:49:56.995
for the conquest of the mainland.

00:49:56.995 --> 00:49:59.870
And one of the things that's very obvious

00:49:59.870 --> 00:50:02.350
from what he writes at the time is that

00:50:02.350 --> 00:50:03.713
there was nothing natural,

00:50:04.953 --> 00:50:06.114
nothing automatic,

00:50:06.114 --> 00:50:10.810
about local people becoming
attracted to the CCP.

00:50:10.810 --> 00:50:13.240
Now, historians might
say, on the one hand,

00:50:13.240 --> 00:50:15.135
that actually, it's fairly obvious,

00:50:15.135 --> 00:50:16.691
there's nothing natural
about that sort of thing.

00:50:16.691 --> 00:50:18.496
But, of course, there is so much language

00:50:18.496 --> 00:50:20.150
about the idea of the masses
being naturally attracted

00:50:20.150 --> 00:50:22.571
to this transformative social force

00:50:22.571 --> 00:50:24.180
that swept away the hated Kuomintang...

00:50:24.180 --> 00:50:26.150
You can fill in the words for yourself.

00:50:26.150 --> 00:50:29.410
It's well worth noting that
the day to day experience was

00:50:29.410 --> 00:50:31.048
clearly often quite different.

00:50:31.048 --> 00:50:34.100
One of many very interesting entries,

00:50:34.100 --> 00:50:35.910
from October 1945,

00:50:35.910 --> 00:50:38.100
is when Yang Siyi takes part in count

00:50:38.100 --> 00:50:40.540
of personnel and weapons,

00:50:40.540 --> 00:50:43.523
because so many people are
deserting at that point

00:50:43.523 --> 00:50:47.550
from the base area that
he's in, in Jinjiang,

00:50:47.550 --> 00:50:52.550
as part of the newly
formed Communist armies.

00:50:52.653 --> 00:50:55.020
They end up at that particular point

00:50:55.020 --> 00:50:57.740
with several hundred people,

00:50:57.740 --> 00:51:00.700
out of about 2,000 troops, deserting.

00:51:00.700 --> 00:51:02.393
Yang observing, as he said,

00:51:02.393 --> 00:51:04.634
"Because of the changes
in their life situation,"

00:51:04.634 --> 00:51:05.680
in other words, being recruited and placed

00:51:05.680 --> 00:51:07.637
in this military formation,

00:51:07.637 --> 00:51:09.340
"everyone got quite worried."

00:51:09.340 --> 00:51:12.011
And then, he adds the following note.

00:51:12.011 --> 00:51:16.268
"The majority are good comrades.

00:51:16.268 --> 00:51:18.996
"There are many reasons for such

00:51:18.996 --> 00:51:20.408
"a serious reduction of numbers,"

00:51:20.408 --> 00:51:21.247
by which he means desertion,

00:51:21.247 --> 00:51:23.889
"but the leaders and the
section chiefs have not united,

00:51:23.889 --> 00:51:27.067
"the warlord residue of
their leadership style

00:51:27.067 --> 00:51:29.450
"and their emotional complicity,"

00:51:29.450 --> 00:51:31.790
i.e dragging people
basically into projects

00:51:31.790 --> 00:51:34.983
that they don't fully
agree with or approve of,

00:51:34.983 --> 00:51:36.677
"are the main reasons.

00:51:36.677 --> 00:51:38.498
"The military leaders and

00:51:38.498 --> 00:51:40.734
"the political commanders
don't get on well,"

00:51:40.734 --> 00:51:42.335
and of course, the
question of political links

00:51:42.335 --> 00:51:44.478
and military links in the top leadership,

00:51:44.478 --> 00:51:45.510
and also the kind of next level down

00:51:45.510 --> 00:51:47.990
of the People's Liberation Army,

00:51:47.990 --> 00:51:51.220
as it formed under that
name and reformed itself,

00:51:51.220 --> 00:51:52.650
is one of the most interesting things

00:51:52.650 --> 00:51:54.676
in terms of how you bring together

00:51:54.676 --> 00:51:55.830
often very disparate groups of people

00:51:55.830 --> 00:51:58.150
and turn them into an
effective fighting force,

00:51:58.150 --> 00:52:00.090
but also political force.

00:52:00.090 --> 00:52:01.738
So he says, "the military leaders

00:52:01.738 --> 00:52:04.128
"and the political
commanders don't get on well,

00:52:04.128 --> 00:52:05.317
"and this reflects abnormality

00:52:05.317 --> 00:52:08.440
"between the party and the masses."

00:52:08.440 --> 00:52:09.630
And I think that's both an interesting

00:52:09.630 --> 00:52:11.936
and indeed, quite honest statement,

00:52:11.936 --> 00:52:14.036
because much of what
then takes place in...

00:52:14.036 --> 00:52:15.160
This is 1945, as you see,

00:52:15.160 --> 00:52:16.935
what happens during much of

00:52:16.935 --> 00:52:18.690
the next two, three, four years is

00:52:18.690 --> 00:52:20.819
a continuing attempt to try

00:52:20.819 --> 00:52:22.429
and make sure that there is some way

00:52:22.429 --> 00:52:24.870
in which as the military
situation improves,

00:52:24.870 --> 00:52:29.500
the political socialization
of those both in the PLA

00:52:29.500 --> 00:52:32.440
and associated with the
PLA becomes much stronger

00:52:32.440 --> 00:52:33.700
because their point of contrast,

00:52:33.700 --> 00:52:35.640
of course, is the increasing collapse

00:52:35.640 --> 00:52:37.340
of the Kuomintang armies,

00:52:37.340 --> 00:52:38.440
and the records that we have

00:52:38.440 --> 00:52:40.890
from people recruited in
those at lower levels are

00:52:40.890 --> 00:52:42.980
very much of increasing realization

00:52:42.980 --> 00:52:44.400
of lack of organization,

00:52:44.400 --> 00:52:45.770
corruption, and failure

00:52:45.770 --> 00:52:49.896
to ultimately achieve their various ends.

00:52:49.896 --> 00:52:54.577
And the monitoring of the political,

00:52:54.577 --> 00:52:57.880
ideological, and indeed,
emotional temperature

00:52:57.880 --> 00:53:02.350
of the charges of a PLA
officer like Yang Siyi is

00:53:03.600 --> 00:53:06.800
of course, very different in
all sorts of obvious ways,

00:53:06.800 --> 00:53:08.580
from the process (speaking
foreign language),

00:53:08.580 --> 00:53:11.700
entry into the party in the present day.

00:53:11.700 --> 00:53:13.280
But the overall aim,

00:53:13.280 --> 00:53:14.680
the aim to try and make sure

00:53:14.680 --> 00:53:16.950
that the political and the emotional

00:53:16.950 --> 00:53:19.300
and the professional
are brought together is

00:53:19.300 --> 00:53:21.440
in some ways a very, very similar one,

00:53:21.440 --> 00:53:23.370
even though today, for the most part,

00:53:23.370 --> 00:53:24.780
it's not in a military context.

00:53:24.780 --> 00:53:25.890
Even though, of course,

00:53:25.890 --> 00:53:28.490
the ethos of militarization is

00:53:28.490 --> 00:53:30.480
one of the things that
we have noted has become

00:53:30.480 --> 00:53:33.640
much stronger in Chinese civil society

00:53:33.640 --> 00:53:37.450
and the media sphere
in the last few years.

00:53:37.450 --> 00:53:39.790
And Yang Siyi himself
also did something that,

00:53:39.790 --> 00:53:42.390
again, from what we know
about party practice today,

00:53:43.800 --> 00:53:45.110
is still very current,

00:53:45.110 --> 00:53:46.744
which is self-reflection.

00:53:46.744 --> 00:53:49.040
His partner was a performer

00:53:49.040 --> 00:53:52.201
in the entertainment division of the PLA

00:53:52.201 --> 00:53:56.363
and the relationship was not
always going terribly well.

00:53:56.363 --> 00:53:58.923
And he writes about himself in his diary,

00:53:58.923 --> 00:54:02.287
"I suddenly felt that
my life was very narrow

00:54:02.287 --> 00:54:03.560
"and quite depressing."

00:54:03.560 --> 00:54:07.120
He says, "these petit bourgeois
feelings being too thick,"

00:54:07.120 --> 00:54:09.957
or too intense, "it's not good.

00:54:09.957 --> 00:54:11.950
"I wanna change that in myself."

00:54:11.950 --> 00:54:14.300
Now, to those of us sitting here

00:54:14.300 --> 00:54:16.280
in an Anglophone country in the 2020s,

00:54:16.280 --> 00:54:18.960
talking about your own petit
bourgeois feelings sounds,

00:54:18.960 --> 00:54:20.760
at best, artificial,

00:54:20.760 --> 00:54:23.220
and possibly even slightly ludicrous.

00:54:23.220 --> 00:54:24.990
The point here is that for someone

00:54:24.990 --> 00:54:26.370
who was feeling something that was

00:54:26.370 --> 00:54:28.750
clearly emotionally quite genuine,

00:54:28.750 --> 00:54:31.420
in this context, the appropriate
way to express it was

00:54:31.420 --> 00:54:33.640
through a particular type of,

00:54:33.640 --> 00:54:34.540
as in this case,

00:54:34.540 --> 00:54:36.740
Marxist-influenced political language

00:54:36.740 --> 00:54:38.390
that helped him to both express,

00:54:38.390 --> 00:54:40.360
and process, and justify,

00:54:40.360 --> 00:54:42.400
what it is that he wanted to do.

00:54:42.400 --> 00:54:44.390
And this became a much
more common practice

00:54:44.390 --> 00:54:47.270
throughout the whole of
the political project

00:54:47.270 --> 00:54:50.010
of that particular era.

00:54:50.010 --> 00:54:54.660
In mid-July of that year, 1946,

00:54:54.660 --> 00:54:57.565
Yang reflected further on what
he called his two big flaws.

00:54:57.565 --> 00:55:00.237
And he says, "When someone hurts

00:55:00.237 --> 00:55:01.727
"my petit bourgeois self regard,

00:55:01.727 --> 00:55:02.917
"I lose patience,

00:55:02.917 --> 00:55:06.226
"or at least I feel unhappy inside."

00:55:06.226 --> 00:55:08.790
The answer, as far as he was concerned,

00:55:08.790 --> 00:55:10.385
was, as he put it,

00:55:10.385 --> 00:55:12.700
"political study and
daily self reflection,"

00:55:12.700 --> 00:55:14.750
which combines both
Marxism and Confucianism,

00:55:14.750 --> 00:55:17.241
I think, in a rather effective way.

00:55:17.241 --> 00:55:19.941
And the idea of course of
(speaking foreign language),

00:55:20.781 --> 00:55:22.350
which Liu Shaoqi, amongst
other top communists,

00:55:22.350 --> 00:55:23.610
was very, very keen on,

00:55:23.610 --> 00:55:24.960
is of course, in many ways,

00:55:24.960 --> 00:55:27.820
an inheritance of that Confucian learning,

00:55:27.820 --> 00:55:30.264
which many of the older leaders of

00:55:30.264 --> 00:55:34.000
the CCP had gone through at that time.

00:55:34.000 --> 00:55:36.220
At one point, as Yang Siyi puts it,

00:55:36.220 --> 00:55:37.437
he says, "We should read books

00:55:37.437 --> 00:55:39.630
"just as often as we sweep the floor."

00:55:39.630 --> 00:55:42.730
And with a rather Mao-like
analogy to bodily function,

00:55:42.730 --> 00:55:44.344
he then adds,

00:55:44.344 --> 00:55:45.640
"I feel that my..."

00:55:45.640 --> 00:55:46.837
This is Yang, again.

00:55:46.837 --> 00:55:49.157
"I feel that petit bourgeois weakness

00:55:49.157 --> 00:55:51.887
"and self regard is like the stinkyness

00:55:51.887 --> 00:55:53.087
"of my toes,

00:55:53.087 --> 00:55:54.860
"it's hard to wash away."

00:55:54.860 --> 00:55:57.800
So while I have no direct primary evidence

00:55:57.800 --> 00:56:00.120
of what Yang Siyi's socks were like,

00:56:00.120 --> 00:56:02.740
I think at least we can
assume that they may have been

00:56:02.740 --> 00:56:05.408
a little overly ideologically fervid

00:56:05.408 --> 00:56:07.167
in this particular case.

00:56:07.167 --> 00:56:09.280
Let me give you also a few thoughts

00:56:09.280 --> 00:56:13.370
from Liu Yanjin, the young woman.

00:56:13.370 --> 00:56:14.730
Born in 1923,

00:56:14.730 --> 00:56:16.701
a member, actually, also one of

00:56:16.701 --> 00:56:18.720
those CCP military performance troops,

00:56:18.720 --> 00:56:20.250
and deeply in love with
a man who actually became

00:56:20.250 --> 00:56:23.237
a rather famous Chinese
movie director, Ling Zifeng,

00:56:23.237 --> 00:56:25.083
who then married someone else.

00:56:26.003 --> 00:56:27.970
And the diary, it is a contemporary diary,

00:56:27.970 --> 00:56:29.410
is very moving in many ways,

00:56:29.410 --> 00:56:32.104
but also, you can tell that it's written

00:56:32.104 --> 00:56:33.060
by someone in her 20s

00:56:33.060 --> 00:56:36.180
at a moment of deep emotional distress.

00:56:36.180 --> 00:56:38.025
One of the moments that she found

00:56:38.025 --> 00:56:39.563
very, very inspiring though was

00:56:39.563 --> 00:56:42.240
aftermath of the Japanese defeat,

00:56:42.240 --> 00:56:44.237
where she says, "(speaking
foreign language),"

00:56:44.237 --> 00:56:45.707
"Oh boy, amazing.

00:56:45.707 --> 00:56:47.490
"Faced with this victory..."

00:56:47.490 --> 00:56:48.610
She's writing to herself,

00:56:48.610 --> 00:56:50.750
she refers to herself
in the second person.

00:56:50.750 --> 00:56:54.537
She says, "the party needs
you to advance more quickly,

00:56:54.537 --> 00:56:56.457
"take on more glorious tasks.

00:56:56.457 --> 00:57:00.220
"It does not need your
pessimistic sighing."

00:57:00.220 --> 00:57:04.987
And later on, she writes
that, in her case,

00:57:04.987 --> 00:57:07.822
"My petit bourgeois romantic fantasies

00:57:07.822 --> 00:57:10.266
"and inappropriate thoughts
should be put on the shelf."

00:57:10.266 --> 00:57:11.310
And she cite there, actually,

00:57:11.310 --> 00:57:14.010
an often quoted, slightly misquoted, take,

00:57:14.010 --> 00:57:16.420
which is the Leninist theory
of the glass of water,

00:57:16.420 --> 00:57:17.280
which you may know,

00:57:17.280 --> 00:57:19.780
which is basically a
slightly parodied version

00:57:19.780 --> 00:57:23.008
that then put forward the
thoughts of Alexandra Kollontai,

00:57:23.008 --> 00:57:25.060
one of the early feminist
Bolshevik thinkers.

00:57:25.060 --> 00:57:28.220
That, supposedly, love and
relationships should be

00:57:28.220 --> 00:57:30.360
as ordinary as drinking a glass of water,

00:57:30.360 --> 00:57:32.267
or drinking from a bottle.

00:57:32.267 --> 00:57:34.840
In other words, in a
truly socialist society,

00:57:34.840 --> 00:57:36.540
it wouldn't become the
source of this huge,

00:57:36.540 --> 00:57:41.540
romantic petit bourgeois
source of distress

00:57:42.065 --> 00:57:46.330
or overly emotional work that it is

00:57:46.330 --> 00:57:47.690
in a petit bourgeois society.

00:57:47.690 --> 00:57:49.040
This is, in fact, a slight parody,

00:57:49.040 --> 00:57:50.520
to be honest, of what Kollontai said,

00:57:50.520 --> 00:57:53.281
but that's not so important
in this particular case.

00:57:53.281 --> 00:57:57.980
And yet when Liu Yanjin's love
affair goes horribly wrong,

00:57:57.980 --> 00:58:00.580
when her intended marry's someone else,

00:58:00.580 --> 00:58:02.310
she writes a furious entry

00:58:02.310 --> 00:58:04.460
in her diary at the time.

00:58:04.460 --> 00:58:07.030
And I think on international
women's day, in fact,

00:58:07.030 --> 00:58:08.440
in March 1946.

00:58:08.440 --> 00:58:10.370
And amongst the things that she writes,

00:58:10.370 --> 00:58:12.370
along the kind of things
that you might expect

00:58:12.370 --> 00:58:14.450
from someone who's been
deeply hurt in love,

00:58:14.450 --> 00:58:17.420
such as "What kind of demon
is troubling your mind?"

00:58:17.420 --> 00:58:19.127
She says, "why don't you have

00:58:19.127 --> 00:58:21.177
"a Bolshevik morality about love?

00:58:21.177 --> 00:58:23.187
"You should use a robust party spirit

00:58:23.187 --> 00:58:24.550
"to evaluate yourself."

00:58:24.550 --> 00:58:26.468
Now, I don't know if you would write

00:58:26.468 --> 00:58:27.600
to your angry lover, I'm not asking,

00:58:27.600 --> 00:58:28.490
words in those terms,

00:58:28.490 --> 00:58:30.590
but again, the fact that at this time,

00:58:30.590 --> 00:58:32.470
in this place, in this era,

00:58:32.470 --> 00:58:34.720
for a 20 something young woman

00:58:34.720 --> 00:58:36.690
deeply, deeply steeped in the way

00:58:36.690 --> 00:58:39.160
in which the party is
recasting its cadres,

00:58:39.160 --> 00:58:40.490
to use this language about something

00:58:40.490 --> 00:58:42.210
that's deeply personal to her

00:58:42.210 --> 00:58:44.030
and deeply, deeply felt,

00:58:44.030 --> 00:58:45.940
and not in any sense inauthentic.

00:58:45.940 --> 00:58:48.510
And yet, of course, encased
in what we would regard

00:58:48.510 --> 00:58:50.520
as Marxist jargon is

00:58:50.520 --> 00:58:52.840
a sign of how important this recasting

00:58:52.840 --> 00:58:57.573
of language was in terms of
people's self-definition.

00:58:59.109 --> 00:59:02.330
Let me conclude, if I may,

00:59:02.330 --> 00:59:05.506
with a few thoughts at the end here

00:59:05.506 --> 00:59:10.208
about where I think some
of this comes together.

00:59:10.208 --> 00:59:15.208
People make propaganda and ideology

00:59:15.670 --> 00:59:17.366
to misquote Marx,

00:59:17.366 --> 00:59:21.987
but they do not make it
exactly as they please.

00:59:21.987 --> 00:59:25.080
In other words, it's not enough

00:59:25.080 --> 00:59:27.280
just to understand, as we must do,

00:59:27.280 --> 00:59:29.040
that the Kuomintang,

00:59:29.040 --> 00:59:30.880
during the period that
we're talking about,

00:59:30.880 --> 00:59:33.643
become immensely downhearted...

00:59:33.643 --> 00:59:37.058
Someone like Wang Shijie,
who we started with,

00:59:37.058 --> 00:59:37.980
just very briefly, in 1947,

00:59:37.980 --> 00:59:39.747
he keeps writing things such as,

00:59:39.747 --> 00:59:40.837
"I feel extremely gloomy

00:59:40.837 --> 00:59:43.247
"about the future of the
current political situation,

00:59:43.247 --> 00:59:45.147
"and the core people
in our party don't have

00:59:45.147 --> 00:59:47.047
"any genuine conviction when it comes

00:59:47.047 --> 00:59:49.230
"to democracy and
constitutional government."

00:59:49.230 --> 00:59:52.741
The CCP diarists of that time have lots

00:59:52.741 --> 00:59:55.520
of quite problematic things
to say, as I mentioned,

00:59:55.520 --> 00:59:57.410
which would probably mean
that they would be guilty

00:59:57.410 --> 00:59:59.460
of historical nihilism in the present day,

00:59:59.460 --> 01:00:00.590
but they don't say that sort of thing.

01:00:00.590 --> 01:00:02.010
There's a genuine emotional register

01:00:02.010 --> 01:00:04.560
of difference between the two sides.

01:00:04.560 --> 01:00:05.907
But beyond that,

01:00:05.907 --> 01:00:07.640
we have to understand how

01:00:07.640 --> 01:00:09.260
the ideological fulfillment

01:00:09.260 --> 01:00:11.500
and sense of self creation links to

01:00:11.500 --> 01:00:13.285
an objective reality.

01:00:13.285 --> 01:00:17.240
And consideration of today's
nationalist propaganda

01:00:17.240 --> 01:00:18.140
and Marxist language,

01:00:18.140 --> 01:00:20.330
and there's plenty of it in China today,

01:00:20.330 --> 01:00:22.690
is often treated as if it's free floating

01:00:22.690 --> 01:00:25.750
and in some ways disconnected
from social realities.

01:00:25.750 --> 01:00:27.140
But I think that is no more true

01:00:27.140 --> 01:00:28.680
in the 2020s

01:00:28.680 --> 01:00:31.580
than it was in the 1940s,

01:00:31.580 --> 01:00:33.841
or indeed visa versa.

01:00:33.841 --> 01:00:35.860
Language that's used today,

01:00:35.860 --> 01:00:37.910
whether it's about
communities of common destiny

01:00:37.910 --> 01:00:40.460
or the desire for China to be

01:00:40.460 --> 01:00:43.006
a 21st century Marxist society,

01:00:43.006 --> 01:00:45.620
is mostly quite inwardly directed.

01:00:45.620 --> 01:00:47.618
It is for a Chinese audience.

01:00:47.618 --> 01:00:49.740
But I think it is still a reminder,

01:00:49.740 --> 01:00:52.740
the fact that it doesn't
translate very well into English

01:00:52.740 --> 01:00:55.300
or into an international audience,

01:00:55.300 --> 01:00:56.450
which is one of the
reasons it doesn't tend

01:00:56.450 --> 01:00:59.450
to get used much in
China's outward propaganda,

01:00:59.450 --> 01:01:02.740
is an nonetheless a useful
and necessary reminder

01:01:02.740 --> 01:01:05.925
that the liberal subject is not the norm

01:01:05.925 --> 01:01:08.804
in all societies at all times.

01:01:08.804 --> 01:01:12.380
In other words, we can't
just translate the documents,

01:01:12.380 --> 01:01:13.700
important though they are,

01:01:13.700 --> 01:01:16.950
we need to understand the
reception of that language

01:01:16.950 --> 01:01:19.840
and the use of it to construct identities

01:01:19.840 --> 01:01:22.446
in certain ways that still have

01:01:22.446 --> 01:01:24.687
a great deal of power.

01:01:24.687 --> 01:01:27.760
That, in the end, I think is why

01:01:27.760 --> 01:01:29.807
that post-war moment,

01:01:29.807 --> 01:01:34.102
1945 to 1949,

01:01:34.102 --> 01:01:37.306
is I think increasingly relevant

01:01:37.306 --> 01:01:41.364
to the moment that China is in today.

01:01:41.364 --> 01:01:44.226
As you see this that I have here,

01:01:44.226 --> 01:01:45.735
I won't go through every
single one of them,

01:01:45.735 --> 01:01:47.280
but in terms of some of
the practical reasons

01:01:47.280 --> 01:01:50.170
why I think the late 1940s has

01:01:50.170 --> 01:01:52.560
an importance for the presence of...

01:01:52.560 --> 01:01:55.250
Sorry, the trajectory of China today,

01:01:55.250 --> 01:01:56.790
its role at the UN,

01:01:56.790 --> 01:01:58.190
its territorial issues,

01:01:58.190 --> 01:02:00.160
the idea of China as a global actor,

01:02:00.160 --> 01:02:03.160
a time of ideological
revolutionary renewal and identity,

01:02:03.160 --> 01:02:04.850
ideas of economic development.

01:02:04.850 --> 01:02:07.300
Moral ballast, the idea of
China as a World War II victor

01:02:07.300 --> 01:02:09.630
that gives it a right in the world today.

01:02:09.630 --> 01:02:11.280
All of these are things that I think,

01:02:11.280 --> 01:02:12.490
in practical terms,

01:02:12.490 --> 01:02:15.810
are important in linking the two periods.

01:02:15.810 --> 01:02:18.358
But I think also, more conceptually,

01:02:18.358 --> 01:02:21.958
the postwar era has one other element

01:02:21.958 --> 01:02:23.740
that is reflected in something

01:02:23.740 --> 01:02:25.970
that is very relevant to today's language.

01:02:25.970 --> 01:02:29.040
It too was on the cusp of a new era,

01:02:29.040 --> 01:02:30.560
one that defined politics,

01:02:30.560 --> 01:02:32.160
or redefined politics,

01:02:32.160 --> 01:02:34.090
in very significant ways.

01:02:34.090 --> 01:02:36.370
And Xi Jinping of course also has defined

01:02:36.370 --> 01:02:38.680
his period in power as a new era,

01:02:38.680 --> 01:02:40.530
albeit one that simultaneously seeks

01:02:40.530 --> 01:02:42.040
some kind of continuity

01:02:42.040 --> 01:02:44.420
with the founding of
the party back in 1921,

01:02:44.420 --> 01:02:47.783
as the resolution of last
November put forward.

01:02:47.783 --> 01:02:51.546
And remembering that in
China, of all places,

01:02:51.546 --> 01:02:54.140
the politics of the future is

01:02:54.140 --> 01:02:56.830
very closely linked to interpretations

01:02:56.830 --> 01:02:59.650
of the recent and quite distant past is,

01:02:59.650 --> 01:03:02.483
I think, one of the many ideas

01:03:02.483 --> 01:03:06.000
that we can draw from, to me,

01:03:06.000 --> 01:03:10.010
that very fascinating, if
very short, postwar period.

01:03:10.010 --> 01:03:12.040
Thank you all very much for your attention

01:03:12.040 --> 01:03:14.440
and delighted to have
some discussion now, Arne.

01:03:15.294 --> 01:03:16.388
Thank you.

01:03:16.388 --> 01:03:19.555
(audience applauding)

01:03:22.922 --> 01:03:23.920
- Thank you very much for

01:03:23.920 --> 01:03:26.270
a wonderful, wonderful presentation.

01:03:26.270 --> 01:03:28.883
Lots of things to discuss.

01:03:28.883 --> 01:03:33.883
While people gather their questions,

01:03:34.010 --> 01:03:35.523
gather their thoughts,

01:03:37.341 --> 01:03:39.170
will I find my glasses so I can see

01:03:39.170 --> 01:03:40.863
who wants to be heard?

01:03:41.962 --> 01:03:45.820
I remember when I first
started looking for,

01:03:45.820 --> 01:03:49.780
and then finding Wang
Shijie's diaries in the...

01:03:49.780 --> 01:03:51.440
I think the (speaking foreign language).

01:03:54.450 --> 01:03:58.130
I thought I had had a new
thesis my PhD dissertation.

01:03:58.130 --> 01:04:00.490
I thought I'd had a major breakthrough

01:04:00.490 --> 01:04:02.680
when I was finally able to locate them

01:04:02.680 --> 01:04:07.680
and then get the family's
permission to actually use them.

01:04:07.687 --> 01:04:11.200
And I came very excited
to the library one morning

01:04:11.200 --> 01:04:14.639
and discovered most of them a written

01:04:14.639 --> 01:04:16.978
in (speaking foreign language),

01:04:16.978 --> 01:04:18.284
so in Engrossed script,

01:04:18.284 --> 01:04:19.640
which, as a graduate student,

01:04:19.640 --> 01:04:21.020
I found...

01:04:21.020 --> 01:04:22.970
Even more so now as a professor, I guess,

01:04:22.970 --> 01:04:25.920
I found really difficult to decipher.

01:04:25.920 --> 01:04:29.069
So I had to call on the help of others

01:04:29.069 --> 01:04:31.260
to try to instruct me in
the particular writing style

01:04:31.260 --> 01:04:33.388
that he used for his diary.

01:04:33.388 --> 01:04:34.221
But when I did get through it,

01:04:34.221 --> 01:04:37.400
I was struck by just
how fascinating those...

01:04:37.400 --> 01:04:40.970
And how personal those
diaries are in many ways.

01:04:40.970 --> 01:04:42.780
Much of which was written was

01:04:42.780 --> 01:04:45.280
when he held high political office.

01:04:45.280 --> 01:04:48.290
But they aren't just reflecting
his political positions

01:04:48.290 --> 01:04:50.120
and his political participation,

01:04:50.120 --> 01:04:52.630
they're also really very introspective

01:04:52.630 --> 01:04:54.110
in terms of what they contribute.

01:04:54.110 --> 01:04:55.861
Maybe I could start you off

01:04:55.861 --> 01:04:57.665
with a question of that, actually, Rana,

01:04:57.665 --> 01:04:58.840
because...

01:04:58.840 --> 01:05:00.830
Or connecting to the
whole issue of identity

01:05:00.830 --> 01:05:01.880
that you bring up here.

01:05:01.880 --> 01:05:04.360
One of the things that I've spent

01:05:04.360 --> 01:05:06.326
quite a bit of time thinking about,

01:05:06.326 --> 01:05:09.228
both when I was writing
on this time period

01:05:09.228 --> 01:05:11.707
but particularly later on,

01:05:11.707 --> 01:05:16.707
is the preoccupation with
the restitution of China

01:05:17.260 --> 01:05:20.390
after the end of the Anti-Japanese War

01:05:20.390 --> 01:05:22.280
as a kind of decolonization,

01:05:22.280 --> 01:05:27.280
that you see presented
both by Republican leaders

01:05:27.323 --> 01:05:30.684
and by communist leaders.

01:05:30.684 --> 01:05:33.290
And this is extremely
interesting in many ways,

01:05:33.290 --> 01:05:35.630
both because it links China

01:05:35.630 --> 01:05:38.080
to a broader the world
of the decolonization

01:05:38.080 --> 01:05:40.460
that was taking place at the same time.

01:05:40.460 --> 01:05:42.890
But it's also really striking in the sense

01:05:42.890 --> 01:05:44.970
that this is the, in many ways,

01:05:44.970 --> 01:05:47.180
the remnants of an empire.

01:05:47.180 --> 01:05:49.977
In the sense that both Republic of China

01:05:49.977 --> 01:05:52.640
and the People's Republic
took over borders

01:05:52.640 --> 01:05:55.604
that were remarkably similar to those

01:05:55.604 --> 01:05:56.577
of the Qing Empire,

01:05:56.577 --> 01:06:00.560
that is connecting itself to
the issue of decolonization.

01:06:00.560 --> 01:06:03.330
So in other words, it isn't decolonization

01:06:03.330 --> 01:06:06.645
by others that needs to be undertaken.

01:06:06.645 --> 01:06:09.286
There is relatively little self-reflection

01:06:09.286 --> 01:06:12.890
in terms of what actually
constitutes the Republic

01:06:12.890 --> 01:06:17.890
that Wang Shijie and anothers
served with such eminence.

01:06:18.180 --> 01:06:20.080
So I wonder if that is also part

01:06:20.080 --> 01:06:23.170
of the identity challenge in a way here,

01:06:23.170 --> 01:06:25.807
then and perhaps also now,

01:06:25.807 --> 01:06:28.510
in terms of the use of concepts.

01:06:28.510 --> 01:06:29.560
Not these illegal terms,

01:06:29.560 --> 01:06:30.393
the imagination,

01:06:31.327 --> 01:06:34.493
or the imaginary of what is
new new challenge would be.

01:06:35.400 --> 01:06:37.610
- It's a great question, Arne, thank you.

01:06:37.610 --> 01:06:39.050
I think one of the best case studies,

01:06:39.050 --> 01:06:41.666
which indeed is mentioned
in Wang Shijie's diary

01:06:41.666 --> 01:06:44.860
but has a wider purchase as well is

01:06:44.860 --> 01:06:46.365
an event, which I think...

01:06:46.365 --> 01:06:47.909
Oh, I think, you know more about it

01:06:47.909 --> 01:06:48.907
than most people I think,

01:06:48.907 --> 01:06:50.470
but which is the independence referendum

01:06:50.470 --> 01:06:53.766
for Outer Mongolia that
takes place very early,

01:06:53.766 --> 01:06:54.720
in late 1945.

01:06:54.720 --> 01:06:56.880
And I think many here
will know, just to say,

01:06:56.880 --> 01:07:00.730
it was organized by
Stalin, on Stalin's behalf.

01:07:00.730 --> 01:07:02.240
And it had a rather miraculous result,

01:07:02.240 --> 01:07:04.830
I think of, sort of 437,000 people

01:07:04.830 --> 01:07:06.370
in Mongolia voted for independence

01:07:06.370 --> 01:07:08.770
and zero people voted against it,

01:07:08.770 --> 01:07:10.187
which was quite--
- Not a single Mongolian.

01:07:10.187 --> 01:07:11.841
- Not a single one,

01:07:11.841 --> 01:07:13.660
so it was entirely free
and fair in every way.

01:07:13.660 --> 01:07:15.960
I'm sure there was soon
to be a similar vote

01:07:15.960 --> 01:07:18.860
in Luhansk and various
other places too, as well.

01:07:18.860 --> 01:07:20.110
But why is this interesting?

01:07:20.110 --> 01:07:23.290
Because apart from the
astonishing nature of the vote,

01:07:23.290 --> 01:07:25.000
which clearly was a gesture of contempt

01:07:25.000 --> 01:07:26.020
by Stalin, in a sense,

01:07:26.020 --> 01:07:29.310
towards the Chinese Republic in that case,

01:07:29.310 --> 01:07:31.225
because for a very long time,

01:07:31.225 --> 01:07:34.118
the Republic of China had continued

01:07:34.118 --> 01:07:36.190
to maintain its claim,

01:07:36.190 --> 01:07:38.660
that was lost in 1911
at the end of the Qing,

01:07:38.660 --> 01:07:40.270
to Outer Mongolia,

01:07:40.270 --> 01:07:42.562
as well as which had gone on essentially

01:07:42.562 --> 01:07:44.280
to become a sort of
Soviet satellite state,

01:07:44.280 --> 01:07:46.460
as a territory that
would have to be restored

01:07:46.460 --> 01:07:48.287
to the Republic in a way that Taiwan

01:07:48.287 --> 01:07:51.750
and various other places would do as well.

01:07:51.750 --> 01:07:53.743
And essentially, of course, it has become

01:07:53.743 --> 01:07:55.040
the only one of those territories

01:07:55.040 --> 01:07:57.770
to which the Chinese state
has essentially given up

01:07:57.770 --> 01:08:00.400
its claim during that period.

01:08:00.400 --> 01:08:03.877
And one of the reasons
it seems to me is that

01:08:03.877 --> 01:08:08.502
a lot of the wider discussion
during that period is

01:08:08.502 --> 01:08:12.324
about what you can...

01:08:12.324 --> 01:08:14.513
Essentially, what you can save

01:08:14.513 --> 01:08:17.305
as part of a central idea of what

01:08:17.305 --> 01:08:18.770
the Chinese Republic is going to be,

01:08:18.770 --> 01:08:20.740
and what has to be let go.

01:08:20.740 --> 01:08:23.650
And having to let go of Mongolia,

01:08:23.650 --> 01:08:27.504
essentially, is a price
that can't be avoided.

01:08:27.504 --> 01:08:29.304
And Wang Shijie himself
knows it's gonna be

01:08:29.304 --> 01:08:31.362
very, very difficult to get past the press

01:08:31.362 --> 01:08:34.000
and some of the more fire brand members

01:08:34.000 --> 01:08:36.100
of the Nationalist Party
don't want to let it go too.

01:08:36.100 --> 01:08:38.627
But because there's a
concern in particular,

01:08:38.627 --> 01:08:39.860
the Northeast and the Northwest,

01:08:39.860 --> 01:08:41.470
So Xinjang and Dongbei, Manchuria,

01:08:41.470 --> 01:08:45.597
are going to come under under threat.

01:08:45.597 --> 01:08:47.620
And in other words, eat away at that

01:08:47.620 --> 01:08:52.620
wider territorial vision of China,

01:08:52.710 --> 01:08:55.280
it's put forward as a
sacrifice that must be made

01:08:55.280 --> 01:08:58.405
to save the rest in that, in that sense.

01:08:58.405 --> 01:09:00.513
There isn't much sense, I think,

01:09:00.513 --> 01:09:03.310
or least I have seen very little sense,

01:09:03.310 --> 01:09:07.210
of the idea that there
should be some reckoning

01:09:07.210 --> 01:09:08.770
with China's own record

01:09:08.770 --> 01:09:10.200
in terms of this period.

01:09:10.200 --> 01:09:12.180
And I'm not entirely surprised,

01:09:12.180 --> 01:09:14.110
simply because one of the things

01:09:14.110 --> 01:09:16.946
that I hope I've drawn
with the picture here,

01:09:16.946 --> 01:09:19.450
and I think is evident in a
sense when one looks at it,

01:09:19.450 --> 01:09:23.670
is that there are so many
different defensive elements

01:09:23.670 --> 01:09:25.980
of trying to construct this state

01:09:25.980 --> 01:09:26.813
at the same time.

01:09:26.813 --> 01:09:29.060
And even these attempts to, I mean...

01:09:29.060 --> 01:09:32.250
In a sense, the appeals
to Molotov and to Burns,

01:09:32.250 --> 01:09:34.397
and to turn up in Paris
and London and say,

01:09:34.397 --> 01:09:37.430
"We should be saying what goes
on with Italy's colonies,"

01:09:37.430 --> 01:09:39.750
is actually part of that exercise too.

01:09:39.750 --> 01:09:41.950
It's part of this wider claim

01:09:41.950 --> 01:09:43.810
that actually, there is this new entity

01:09:43.810 --> 01:09:44.930
that is a postwar China

01:09:44.930 --> 01:09:47.400
that has a right to do
this, that, and the other.

01:09:47.400 --> 01:09:49.120
I think there's also,

01:09:49.120 --> 01:09:50.330
and this I'm stretching slightly

01:09:50.330 --> 01:09:53.285
because I don't, at the
moment, have a particular quote

01:09:53.285 --> 01:09:54.945
I could put my hand on to do that,

01:09:54.945 --> 01:09:56.623
although perhaps I should look for one,

01:09:56.623 --> 01:09:58.080
but I suspect there's a wrong feeling that

01:09:58.080 --> 01:10:02.360
if the British and the
French do not resile

01:10:02.360 --> 01:10:04.550
in any sense from their empires,

01:10:04.550 --> 01:10:05.970
and India aside,

01:10:05.970 --> 01:10:07.860
they really don't during this period,

01:10:07.860 --> 01:10:10.470
and that is something that
is observed quite strongly

01:10:10.470 --> 01:10:12.610
by the Chinese during this period.

01:10:12.610 --> 01:10:15.350
Then the sense that looking at
the moat in their own eye is

01:10:15.350 --> 01:10:17.800
a useful task at this point, I think,

01:10:17.800 --> 01:10:19.790
is probably not something that's regarded

01:10:19.790 --> 01:10:24.120
as a great political priority
at this particular stage.

01:10:24.120 --> 01:10:25.180
But I mean, I think you're right

01:10:25.180 --> 01:10:27.500
that it is a...

01:10:27.500 --> 01:10:30.310
While there is lots of
insight on various things,

01:10:30.310 --> 01:10:32.590
the sense of reflexiveness on the nature

01:10:32.590 --> 01:10:35.190
of what exactly the
Republic is supposed to be

01:10:35.190 --> 01:10:37.360
almost isn't given enough space

01:10:37.360 --> 01:10:39.493
to breathe during that time, I think.

01:10:40.803 --> 01:10:42.505
- It is quite when you think about it,

01:10:42.505 --> 01:10:45.890
connected to other periods of transition

01:10:45.890 --> 01:10:46.723
after great wars.

01:10:46.723 --> 01:10:49.590
I mean, those was who read Putin's speech

01:10:51.300 --> 01:10:55.897
in which justifies the
takeover by violence

01:10:55.897 --> 01:10:58.780
or by armed force of Ukraine,

01:10:58.780 --> 01:11:02.730
spends an enormous amount
of time castigating Lenin

01:11:02.730 --> 01:11:05.410
and, to some extent, Stalin as well

01:11:05.410 --> 01:11:06.860
for having done just that.

01:11:06.860 --> 01:11:09.110
For thinking about Russia as an empire

01:11:09.110 --> 01:11:11.310
in which people who were not central

01:11:11.310 --> 01:11:12.950
to the empire actually had rights.

01:11:12.950 --> 01:11:15.200
but you almost never
find that on the Chinese

01:11:16.613 --> 01:11:20.480
(indistinct) Chinese Republic.

01:11:20.480 --> 01:11:21.580
Okay, other questions?

01:11:24.720 --> 01:11:25.553
Yes.

01:11:26.980 --> 01:11:29.093
- [Audience Member] Hey
Rana, thank you very much.

01:11:29.093 --> 01:11:31.435
So a non-historian facile question.

01:11:31.435 --> 01:11:34.672
It seems to me you've skated very lightly

01:11:34.672 --> 01:11:37.435
over the fact that we're
talking about nationalists,

01:11:37.435 --> 01:11:39.693
not communists (indistinct).

01:11:39.693 --> 01:11:42.491
You seem to make big, bold claim here

01:11:42.491 --> 01:11:44.003
about cultural continuity

01:11:44.003 --> 01:11:46.746
(indistinct) the political situation,

01:11:46.746 --> 01:11:48.168
historical narrative, and so on,

01:11:48.168 --> 01:11:52.127
being far more important
than socialist mindsets,

01:11:52.127 --> 01:11:54.090
political views and everything else,

01:11:54.090 --> 01:11:55.703
in making these comparisons

01:11:55.703 --> 01:11:57.870
between the '40s and 2022.

01:11:59.424 --> 01:12:01.085
Any comments?

01:12:01.085 --> 01:12:05.123
- Actually, I thought I was
rather doing the opposite,

01:12:05.123 --> 01:12:07.128
which is pointing out that, actually,

01:12:07.128 --> 01:12:08.900
language and mindset makes
a huge amount of difference

01:12:08.900 --> 01:12:10.210
at this time,

01:12:10.210 --> 01:12:12.250
because the sort of
language that you would get

01:12:12.250 --> 01:12:13.910
amongst these younger,

01:12:13.910 --> 01:12:15.710
and not so young cadres,

01:12:15.710 --> 01:12:17.250
is not at all the kind of language

01:12:17.250 --> 01:12:18.900
that would've been used in that form

01:12:18.900 --> 01:12:21.850
by the kind of figures
on the nationalist side,

01:12:21.850 --> 01:12:23.150
who have their own political language

01:12:23.150 --> 01:12:24.724
and very strong...

01:12:24.724 --> 01:12:25.557
People like Chiang Ching-kuo,

01:12:25.557 --> 01:12:26.480
or indeed, Wang Shijie,

01:12:26.480 --> 01:12:28.443
or actually, Jiǎng Tíngfú,

01:12:28.443 --> 01:12:29.825
or Chen Bulei,

01:12:29.825 --> 01:12:32.900
Chiang Kai-shek's personal
secretary, in a sense,

01:12:32.900 --> 01:12:35.485
who eventually takes his own life in 1948

01:12:35.485 --> 01:12:38.100
through a complication of depression

01:12:38.100 --> 01:12:41.420
that comes from personal
mental health issues,

01:12:41.420 --> 01:12:43.870
but also with the collapse of the regime

01:12:43.870 --> 01:12:45.845
around him at that time.

01:12:45.845 --> 01:12:48.721
But much of the language
in which they express

01:12:48.721 --> 01:12:52.450
their own growing sense
of gloom and despair is a,

01:12:52.450 --> 01:12:53.723
as you would know, Chloe, very well,

01:12:53.723 --> 01:12:57.350
a quite traditional sort of
Confucian self-presentation

01:12:57.350 --> 01:12:59.850
that's, in some ways, rather rejecting

01:12:59.850 --> 01:13:02.520
of this idea of self-realization

01:13:02.520 --> 01:13:04.340
and changing through ideas

01:13:04.340 --> 01:13:09.340
that come from a Marxist
framework in that sense.

01:13:09.410 --> 01:13:11.530
I think that one of the things

01:13:11.530 --> 01:13:13.950
that could do with more emphasis

01:13:13.950 --> 01:13:15.300
during this particular period,

01:13:15.300 --> 01:13:16.860
cause it's one of the rare periods

01:13:17.815 --> 01:13:20.009
when you get not quite a even conflict,

01:13:20.009 --> 01:13:21.211
but certainly one where there's

01:13:21.211 --> 01:13:23.150
strong elements on both sides,

01:13:23.150 --> 01:13:27.250
is the way in which the
post war period is one

01:13:27.250 --> 01:13:29.170
of different sorts of political visions

01:13:29.170 --> 01:13:32.090
that are expressed in different
sorts of political language

01:13:32.090 --> 01:13:33.520
to socialize the people

01:13:33.520 --> 01:13:35.145
who are putting them forward.

01:13:35.145 --> 01:13:35.978
And one of the areas
that I didn't talk about,

01:13:35.978 --> 01:13:38.129
partly 'cause I thought
you wouldn't all thank me

01:13:38.129 --> 01:13:39.154
for going on for three hours,

01:13:39.154 --> 01:13:40.697
but there's quite a lot
about land reform, actually,

01:13:40.697 --> 01:13:42.560
the (speaking foreign language)
program during this time,

01:13:42.560 --> 01:13:44.610
where actually lots of
these people are recording,

01:13:44.610 --> 01:13:46.470
first of all, really interesting things

01:13:46.470 --> 01:13:48.240
about how, again...

01:13:48.240 --> 01:13:50.930
In retrospect, it's all
about the peasants rise up

01:13:50.930 --> 01:13:52.190
against their evil oppressors,

01:13:52.190 --> 01:13:53.830
and there's actually
plenty of that going on,

01:13:53.830 --> 01:13:56.110
but also, a great deal
of it is spent in a way

01:13:56.110 --> 01:13:57.760
that isn't always so much stressed

01:13:57.760 --> 01:14:01.090
in communists basically giving
other communists a hard time

01:14:01.090 --> 01:14:05.250
about not being sufficiently
indoctrinated into the program

01:14:05.250 --> 01:14:06.900
and having the right kind of...

01:14:06.900 --> 01:14:08.060
The wrong kind of rightist

01:14:08.060 --> 01:14:11.290
or the right kind of leftist
viewpoint as part of that.

01:14:11.290 --> 01:14:12.730
But the consequence of
these things were not

01:14:12.730 --> 01:14:14.550
by any means purely abstract.

01:14:14.550 --> 01:14:16.740
They ended in real people being locked up

01:14:16.740 --> 01:14:18.570
or hit with bill hooks,

01:14:18.570 --> 01:14:23.430
or murdered at their
neighbors in front of the...

01:14:25.350 --> 01:14:27.310
In front of other farmers.

01:14:27.310 --> 01:14:29.500
So these things have a real context

01:14:29.500 --> 01:14:31.068
in that sense.

01:14:31.068 --> 01:14:32.847
Does that get to the question

01:14:32.847 --> 01:14:34.004
or was that to so...

01:14:34.004 --> 01:14:35.254
Yup, yup, yup.

01:14:37.754 --> 01:14:38.837
- [Arne] Yes.

01:14:39.937 --> 01:14:40.953
- [Audience Member] Thank you for this

01:14:40.953 --> 01:14:42.293
really stimulating talk,

01:14:42.293 --> 01:14:43.340
I really enjoyed it.

01:14:43.340 --> 01:14:45.551
I wanted to ask you maybe to reflect

01:14:45.551 --> 01:14:47.870
a bit more on this concept
of the Chinese postwar,

01:14:47.870 --> 01:14:49.907
and particularly the end date for it,

01:14:50.986 --> 01:14:53.631
which it sounds like
you're sticking with 1949.

01:14:53.631 --> 01:14:56.075
- Oh no, no, no, no,
no, no, absolutely not.

01:14:56.075 --> 01:14:57.612
So thank you for (indistinct) that.

01:14:57.612 --> 01:14:58.445
But sorry, finish your question.

01:14:58.445 --> 01:15:01.172
- [Audience Member] Maybe that's
enough to let you go there

01:15:01.172 --> 01:15:02.420
'cause I was wondering, first,

01:15:02.420 --> 01:15:04.350
how much it does actually
differ from the civil war.

01:15:04.350 --> 01:15:06.448
Is this just another way of thinking

01:15:06.448 --> 01:15:07.281
about the civil war period, (indistinct)?

01:15:08.148 --> 01:15:08.981
But then the other part,

01:15:08.981 --> 01:15:10.500
I'm a Japan specialist

01:15:10.500 --> 01:15:13.265
and I've been thinking
comparatively as you were talking,

01:15:13.265 --> 01:15:14.905
and one thing that I think comes up

01:15:14.905 --> 01:15:16.171
a lot in the Japanese case is

01:15:16.171 --> 01:15:18.030
the endless debates about when
the postwar actually ends?

01:15:18.030 --> 01:15:20.160
- Yes, when does (speaking
foreign language) actually end?

01:15:20.160 --> 01:15:21.849
Do you have the answer to that?

01:15:21.849 --> 01:15:22.844
'Cause I don't.

01:15:22.844 --> 01:15:24.014
- [Audience Member] Well,
and I think, one thing,

01:15:24.014 --> 01:15:24.847
it varies a lot by geography.

01:15:24.847 --> 01:15:26.350
Like, Okinawa's postwar is very different

01:15:26.350 --> 01:15:27.880
than mainland Japan's postwar,

01:15:27.880 --> 01:15:30.270
and Sakhalin and the
Kuril Islands' postwar is

01:15:30.270 --> 01:15:32.790
still ongoing (indistinct) or whatever.

01:15:32.790 --> 01:15:34.850
But it's also a historical question

01:15:34.850 --> 01:15:36.090
that comes up again again, again.

01:15:36.090 --> 01:15:37.830
Is it the occupation?

01:15:37.830 --> 01:15:39.408
Is it the Olympics?

01:15:39.408 --> 01:15:40.430
Is it the oil crisis?

01:15:40.430 --> 01:15:41.622
Is it the death of the (indistinct)?

01:15:41.622 --> 01:15:42.905
And so on and so forth.

01:15:42.905 --> 01:15:44.070
And so, I'm wondering,

01:15:44.070 --> 01:15:45.753
maybe that's too much to, I realize,

01:15:45.753 --> 01:15:46.800
(indistinct) trying to
address all of that,

01:15:46.800 --> 01:15:49.613
but I was wondering how much you see 1949,

01:15:49.613 --> 01:15:50.810
or perhaps some other date,

01:15:50.810 --> 01:15:54.050
as a moment when kind of
all of the stuff gets put

01:15:54.050 --> 01:15:57.532
on hold for a while and we
enter kind of a different period

01:15:57.532 --> 01:15:58.365
of the Cultural Revolution

01:15:58.365 --> 01:16:00.428
or the Maoist period or whatever,

01:16:00.428 --> 01:16:01.261
or whether you see sort of continuities

01:16:01.261 --> 01:16:02.760
in certain areas of China

01:16:02.760 --> 01:16:04.654
or certain areas of Chinese politics?

01:16:04.654 --> 01:16:06.364
That's very open ended, I've realized,

01:16:06.364 --> 01:16:07.855
but if you could talk
a bit more about that,

01:16:07.855 --> 01:16:08.688
I'd appreciate it.

01:16:08.688 --> 01:16:09.521
- No, I'd be very happy to do that,

01:16:09.521 --> 01:16:10.923
and it's a great question.

01:16:10.923 --> 01:16:11.756
I mean, just as...

01:16:11.756 --> 01:16:12.589
I mean, I think one level,

01:16:12.589 --> 01:16:13.730
just as you've said, the
Japanese (indistinct),

01:16:13.730 --> 01:16:16.180
or indeed the European
postwar doesn't have

01:16:16.180 --> 01:16:17.420
an obvious endpoint,

01:16:17.420 --> 01:16:22.420
and indeed, whether the Russian
assault on Ukraine is going

01:16:23.050 --> 01:16:24.924
to prove, as many people are saying,

01:16:24.924 --> 01:16:26.747
the end of a particular
sort of postwar order is

01:16:26.747 --> 01:16:27.580
something we're debating,

01:16:27.580 --> 01:16:30.003
as we speak right to right now.

01:16:30.003 --> 01:16:33.116
I think the answer is it's certainly...

01:16:33.116 --> 01:16:35.530
I personally don't intend it to be 1949.

01:16:35.530 --> 01:16:37.223
It's not meant to be literally

01:16:37.223 --> 01:16:38.870
just the four years
(murmurs) after period.

01:16:38.870 --> 01:16:40.630
But I do think of it as something

01:16:40.630 --> 01:16:42.400
that obviously has the
civil war at heart of it,

01:16:42.400 --> 01:16:43.950
because how could it not?

01:16:43.950 --> 01:16:45.550
But that stretches beyond that,

01:16:45.550 --> 01:16:47.640
at least into the mid-1950s.

01:16:47.640 --> 01:16:49.143
I think the idea of it is this,

01:16:49.143 --> 01:16:51.430
that the forces that are unleashed

01:16:51.430 --> 01:16:54.530
by the circumstances of the very long war

01:16:54.530 --> 01:16:58.186
with Japan are not worked through,

01:16:58.186 --> 01:17:00.940
certainly by 1949.

01:17:00.940 --> 01:17:02.539
I mean, in a sense,

01:17:02.539 --> 01:17:04.224
many of the things that
emerge at that point,

01:17:04.224 --> 01:17:06.170
not least the split between
the mainland and Taiwan,

01:17:06.170 --> 01:17:08.800
are products of the
forces that are unleashed

01:17:08.800 --> 01:17:10.010
at that particular time.

01:17:10.010 --> 01:17:13.310
But one of the things that I
think new work is beginning to

01:17:13.310 --> 01:17:15.250
really get to grips with is

01:17:15.250 --> 01:17:17.465
the idea that, in some ways,

01:17:17.465 --> 01:17:20.520
the fact that you have
these split jurisdictions

01:17:20.520 --> 01:17:23.566
and legitimacies between
the mainland and Taiwan

01:17:23.566 --> 01:17:25.707
really matter in some
quite important ways.

01:17:25.707 --> 01:17:27.090
I mean, I think I can say,

01:17:27.090 --> 01:17:29.440
without specifically (murmurs) things,

01:17:29.440 --> 01:17:30.273
but I think there's
gonna be a postdoc coming

01:17:30.273 --> 01:17:32.280
to Arne's program during next year

01:17:32.280 --> 01:17:35.040
whose doctoral thesis I
examined at Cambridge University

01:17:35.040 --> 01:17:37.030
and is a fascinating study of,

01:17:37.030 --> 01:17:39.010
in particular, the way
in which various types

01:17:39.010 --> 01:17:42.030
of communist and anti
communist legitimacy are sought

01:17:42.030 --> 01:17:43.020
in the global south,

01:17:43.020 --> 01:17:45.040
or third world, you might say,

01:17:45.040 --> 01:17:47.530
by Taiwan and the PRC.

01:17:47.530 --> 01:17:49.250
I think the idea that perhaps existed

01:17:49.250 --> 01:17:50.956
even a little while ago

01:17:50.956 --> 01:17:52.130
that once it was on the island,

01:17:52.130 --> 01:17:53.510
the Taiwan government had no time

01:17:53.510 --> 01:17:55.841
or interest in anything except trying

01:17:55.841 --> 01:17:56.674
to shore up its own position

01:17:56.674 --> 01:17:58.200
and oppressing dissidents;

01:17:58.200 --> 01:18:01.890
both of which it did do to
a very extensive extent.

01:18:01.890 --> 01:18:04.470
But also, had really quite
a strong idea of itself

01:18:04.470 --> 01:18:06.050
as an actor in the world,

01:18:06.050 --> 01:18:08.490
an actor that was part of
an anti-communist front,

01:18:08.490 --> 01:18:11.338
an actor that was still
a postcolonial actor

01:18:11.338 --> 01:18:12.925
in its own right.

01:18:12.925 --> 01:18:14.740
So hence the connections with all

01:18:14.740 --> 01:18:16.160
these slightly dodgy folk,

01:18:16.160 --> 01:18:19.040
likes Syngman Rhee and
Bao Dao, and so forth,

01:18:19.040 --> 01:18:21.145
continue to be important.

01:18:21.145 --> 01:18:21.978
And also, actually,

01:18:21.978 --> 01:18:24.319
an alternative economic actor as well.

01:18:24.319 --> 01:18:26.390
One of the great ironies of the postwar is

01:18:26.390 --> 01:18:30.819
that land reform under Chiang
Kai-Shek goes really well.

01:18:30.819 --> 01:18:32.400
It's just, it goes really well on Taiwan

01:18:32.400 --> 01:18:33.570
with American support,

01:18:33.570 --> 01:18:35.620
once he's actually got
away from the mainland,

01:18:35.620 --> 01:18:38.380
where it'd have been an
absolute disaster on his part.

01:18:38.380 --> 01:18:40.990
And land reform of very different sort is

01:18:40.990 --> 01:18:43.310
then offered in the mainland

01:18:43.310 --> 01:18:44.540
as a challenge to Asia,

01:18:44.540 --> 01:18:46.210
and indeed beyond Asia, as well,

01:18:46.210 --> 01:18:49.130
as in Vietnam and other parts of

01:18:49.130 --> 01:18:51.620
an emergent revolutionary
in Southeast Asia.

01:18:51.620 --> 01:18:54.303
Even for some time into the 1950s,

01:18:54.303 --> 01:18:58.630
the colonial challenge
to the Republic of China,

01:18:58.630 --> 01:19:00.680
as indeed to the People's
Republic is still there.

01:19:00.680 --> 01:19:04.222
Malaysia doesn't become
independent until 1957.

01:19:04.222 --> 01:19:06.630
You have Vietnam Wars,

01:19:06.630 --> 01:19:08.699
first with the French, and
then with the Americans,

01:19:08.699 --> 01:19:10.920
and then indeed with
the Chinese, eventually.

01:19:10.920 --> 01:19:12.780
In other words, all of the agendas

01:19:12.780 --> 01:19:14.690
that shape the global postwar,

01:19:14.690 --> 01:19:17.680
or even the global Cold
War, I suppose, Arne,

01:19:17.680 --> 01:19:21.770
continue to be of relevance
to the political projects

01:19:21.770 --> 01:19:24.670
that are underway both
in the mainland of China

01:19:24.670 --> 01:19:25.760
and in Taiwan,

01:19:25.760 --> 01:19:27.710
even if they're not
always of equal valency

01:19:27.710 --> 01:19:29.040
and equal significance.

01:19:29.040 --> 01:19:30.330
But they both matter at that time

01:19:30.330 --> 01:19:32.270
because they're in that world,

01:19:32.270 --> 01:19:33.470
making the case,

01:19:33.470 --> 01:19:35.300
and also pushing forward agendas

01:19:35.300 --> 01:19:38.200
that clearly suit them better than...

01:19:38.200 --> 01:19:41.480
Better than their opponent.

01:19:41.480 --> 01:19:43.361
- [Arne] That is a person that

01:19:43.361 --> 01:19:44.194
a personality discussion in itself.

01:19:44.194 --> 01:19:45.758
Yes.

01:19:45.758 --> 01:19:48.676
- [Audience Member] Thank
you so much for that talk.

01:19:48.676 --> 01:19:51.099
(indistinct) two questions.

01:19:51.099 --> 01:19:53.160
The first question regarding your...

01:19:53.160 --> 01:19:55.040
I think the question regarding

01:19:55.040 --> 01:19:57.087
the (indistinct).

01:19:57.087 --> 01:19:58.760
And I was wondering,

01:19:58.760 --> 01:20:01.190
I'm very much convinced of their, I guess,

01:20:01.190 --> 01:20:03.678
the self-reflection
they're trying to practice

01:20:03.678 --> 01:20:04.739
in their Republic.

01:20:04.739 --> 01:20:08.543
The question is, how much do
we see this kind of process

01:20:08.543 --> 01:20:11.399
as they're being part of the product

01:20:11.399 --> 01:20:12.232
from the young years,

01:20:12.232 --> 01:20:14.981
when the young campaign
was just kind of started

01:20:14.981 --> 01:20:16.350
in '42 to '45?

01:20:16.350 --> 01:20:20.660
And given that, I think there
has this part of continuity,

01:20:20.660 --> 01:20:23.660
I was wondering, how much can we see

01:20:23.660 --> 01:20:25.499
as kind of independent of the

01:20:25.499 --> 01:20:29.117
(indistinct) process themselves,

01:20:29.117 --> 01:20:32.270
or gradually kind of a
byproduct of the process

01:20:32.270 --> 01:20:33.800
during the Yang campaign

01:20:33.800 --> 01:20:38.217
and during the kind of the (indistinct)?

01:20:38.217 --> 01:20:39.860
The second question is, I feel,

01:20:39.860 --> 01:20:42.160
I guess, you have been discussing

01:20:42.160 --> 01:20:46.217
the nationalist for (indistinct)

01:20:46.217 --> 01:20:49.079
and all sorts the communist cultures,

01:20:49.079 --> 01:20:51.401
and I wonder, during the postwar,

01:20:51.401 --> 01:20:54.613
there's also the third pass intellectual,

01:20:54.613 --> 01:20:55.953
they're kind of debates,

01:20:55.953 --> 01:20:58.222
(indistinct) the future of China--

01:20:58.222 --> 01:21:00.674
- So Zhang Junmai, and
those sorts of people.

01:21:00.674 --> 01:21:02.198
- [Audience Member] The question is like,

01:21:02.198 --> 01:21:05.380
how do I say less kind
of intellectual debates

01:21:05.380 --> 01:21:08.720
as part of, I guess,
the legacy of continuity

01:21:08.720 --> 01:21:09.897
that we have?

01:21:09.897 --> 01:21:13.158
(indistinct)?

01:21:13.158 --> 01:21:15.602
- You have two great questions, thanks.

01:21:15.602 --> 01:21:16.795
Well, let me try and briefly...

01:21:16.795 --> 01:21:18.301
Sorry, I know we're
getting to time as well,

01:21:18.301 --> 01:21:19.497
but I'll try and give a brief answer

01:21:19.497 --> 01:21:20.502
to each of them.

01:21:20.502 --> 01:21:21.335
I think, in the first...

01:21:21.335 --> 01:21:22.585
And I think you're absolutely right.

01:21:22.585 --> 01:21:24.016
I don't think with these sorts of very,

01:21:24.016 --> 01:21:26.080
very strongly committed revolutionaries.

01:21:26.080 --> 01:21:27.850
It certainly doesn't begin
in the postwar period.

01:21:27.850 --> 01:21:29.630
They have this very long engagement

01:21:29.630 --> 01:21:32.280
with the revolution from
their teenage years,

01:21:32.280 --> 01:21:34.190
from the 1920s and 30s onwards.

01:21:34.190 --> 01:21:36.897
And again, I didn't have remotely

01:21:36.897 --> 01:21:38.667
enough time to talk about
it, but each of the...

01:21:38.667 --> 01:21:40.005
Well, in particular, Zeng Zhi,

01:21:40.005 --> 01:21:40.950
but also, to some extent, Liu Yanjin,

01:21:40.950 --> 01:21:44.579
they have long and quite
complex personal lives,

01:21:44.579 --> 01:21:46.630
as with all revolutionaries

01:21:46.630 --> 01:21:49.206
that are linked with their
revolutionary lives as well.

01:21:49.206 --> 01:21:50.680
And one of the elements, again,

01:21:50.680 --> 01:21:52.106
that I only talked about in passing,

01:21:52.106 --> 01:21:52.997
but in the study that I'm doing, I think,

01:21:52.997 --> 01:21:56.160
has in a sense become
more important for me is

01:21:56.160 --> 01:21:57.460
the perhaps not surprising,

01:21:57.460 --> 01:21:58.320
and somewhat depressing,

01:21:58.320 --> 01:22:01.950
but nonetheless necessary discovery,

01:22:01.950 --> 01:22:03.730
historically necessary discovery,

01:22:03.730 --> 01:22:06.750
that gender really makes a difference.

01:22:06.750 --> 01:22:09.240
The experience of young
revolutionary women

01:22:09.240 --> 01:22:10.970
in the 1930s and 40s,

01:22:10.970 --> 01:22:12.600
even ones like Zeng Zhi,

01:22:12.600 --> 01:22:14.570
who run off in their teenage years

01:22:14.570 --> 01:22:18.420
and are taking part in some
pretty violent activities,

01:22:18.420 --> 01:22:20.550
are still given a much harder time

01:22:20.550 --> 01:22:22.500
by party leaders than young men

01:22:22.500 --> 01:22:23.600
in that particular position,

01:22:23.600 --> 01:22:25.690
and that becomes visible
over and over again.

01:22:25.690 --> 01:22:27.963
So I think in terms of that structure,

01:22:27.963 --> 01:22:28.890
I'd say it's certainly
very long term thing,

01:22:28.890 --> 01:22:31.899
but actually, gender is yet
another one of the frameworks

01:22:31.899 --> 01:22:33.570
that I think shapes that experience,

01:22:33.570 --> 01:22:35.220
and that's why I thought
it was very important

01:22:35.220 --> 01:22:36.880
to make sure that at least

01:22:36.880 --> 01:22:38.673
a significant amount of the
revolutionary experience

01:22:38.673 --> 01:22:40.610
that one considers during this period is

01:22:40.610 --> 01:22:43.120
from women as well as from men.

01:22:43.120 --> 01:22:45.639
In terms of that third
force of intellectuals,

01:22:45.639 --> 01:22:47.650
I mean, I've been doing some reading

01:22:47.650 --> 01:22:48.483
on some of the people...

01:22:48.483 --> 01:22:50.161
One of the people who, again,

01:22:50.161 --> 01:22:51.177
is very well known,

01:22:51.177 --> 01:22:54.420
but sometimes some of his
specifics are not always studied

01:22:54.420 --> 01:22:56.361
as they might, partly
cause he wrote so much,

01:22:56.361 --> 01:22:57.400
is Liang Shu-ming.

01:22:57.400 --> 01:22:59.298
And Liang Shu-ming, he...

01:22:59.298 --> 01:23:00.131
Because in a sense,

01:23:00.131 --> 01:23:02.060
for English-speaking scholarly readers,

01:23:02.060 --> 01:23:04.420
he's been characterized
as "The Last Confucian,"

01:23:04.420 --> 01:23:06.236
Guy Alitto's famous book.

01:23:06.236 --> 01:23:09.140
But in a sense, he is that,

01:23:09.140 --> 01:23:12.100
but he's much more as
well, in various ways.

01:23:12.100 --> 01:23:13.180
And one of the interesting things,

01:23:13.180 --> 01:23:14.820
and again, Arne, because
you know everything

01:23:14.820 --> 01:23:15.867
on (indistinct),

01:23:15.867 --> 01:23:18.339
but when Liang Shu-ming takes this flight

01:23:18.339 --> 01:23:21.096
to meet Mao and these other guys

01:23:21.096 --> 01:23:23.000
while they're still in Yenan, about 1946,

01:23:23.000 --> 01:23:24.559
and sort of has a long chat with them.

01:23:24.559 --> 01:23:26.039
And there's this wonderful scene

01:23:26.039 --> 01:23:27.360
in which Liang Shu-ming
explains, in great detail,

01:23:27.360 --> 01:23:29.680
this very, very complex political system

01:23:29.680 --> 01:23:31.448
that he thinks is going to navigate

01:23:31.448 --> 01:23:35.040
between Western-style government,

01:23:35.040 --> 01:23:36.680
which he doesn't think
is gonna work in China,

01:23:36.680 --> 01:23:37.890
and a Bolshevik-style government,

01:23:37.890 --> 01:23:39.270
which he doesn't think
is gonna work in China,

01:23:39.270 --> 01:23:40.190
and the Kuomintang,

01:23:40.190 --> 01:23:42.320
which he hates as well and
thinks that's not gonna work.

01:23:42.320 --> 01:23:43.680
And it involves lots...

01:23:43.680 --> 01:23:45.550
He makes comparisons with what's happening

01:23:45.550 --> 01:23:46.540
in the United Nations,

01:23:46.540 --> 01:23:48.210
which is just being set up at that time,

01:23:48.210 --> 01:23:50.542
and what you can take from various

01:23:50.542 --> 01:23:51.860
traditional Chinese things.

01:23:51.860 --> 01:23:53.716
Anyway, takes a long time.

01:23:53.716 --> 01:23:54.861
He's talking to Mao Zedong,

01:23:54.861 --> 01:23:55.865
he's talking to Zhu De,

01:23:55.865 --> 01:23:56.698
he's talking to lots of top people.

01:23:56.698 --> 01:23:58.419
And then at the end, he stops,

01:23:58.419 --> 01:24:00.381
and this is his own recollection of it,

01:24:00.381 --> 01:24:01.214
so he's telling against himself.

01:24:01.214 --> 01:24:02.830
And there's just silence,

01:24:02.830 --> 01:24:05.690
and all these top communist
leaders are staring at him.

01:24:05.690 --> 01:24:09.117
And then, finally, I think
it's actually Zhu De, says,

01:24:09.117 --> 01:24:12.881
"Ah, so it took you 30 years
to work that out, did it?"

01:24:12.881 --> 01:24:16.210
And they turn onto other
subjects quite quickly.

01:24:16.210 --> 01:24:18.780
So there's definitely
a willingness to hear,

01:24:18.780 --> 01:24:21.290
but whether or not people actually react

01:24:21.290 --> 01:24:22.400
to some of these people, I think,

01:24:22.400 --> 01:24:25.610
unfortunately is not as
obvious in that case.

01:24:25.610 --> 01:24:26.880
But it's interesting to see the efforts

01:24:26.880 --> 01:24:28.817
that they made.

01:24:28.817 --> 01:24:31.010
And I think that they were
by no means, as really,

01:24:31.010 --> 01:24:34.059
as needful of being dismissed
as they sometimes are.

01:24:34.059 --> 01:24:35.550
They were an important part
of a much richer debate

01:24:35.550 --> 01:24:38.119
than sometimes realized.

01:24:38.119 --> 01:24:40.419
- [Arne] I think we'll
take one more question,

01:24:42.147 --> 01:24:43.160
if there's one.

01:24:43.160 --> 01:24:45.113
Let us go with two.

01:24:46.589 --> 01:24:48.794
(indistinct), first.

01:24:48.794 --> 01:24:52.877
- [Audience Member] (indistinct)

01:24:56.618 --> 01:24:59.359
- I'm just gonna come
forward a little bit.

01:24:59.359 --> 01:25:00.781
- [Audience Member] Sorry,
I need to speak louder.

01:25:00.781 --> 01:25:02.238
So I think it's fascinating that

01:25:02.238 --> 01:25:03.460
you were able to work with a lot of

01:25:03.460 --> 01:25:04.720
personal writings and diaries.

01:25:04.720 --> 01:25:06.778
And my question is,

01:25:06.778 --> 01:25:10.882
because I know diary writing
for that time period is

01:25:10.882 --> 01:25:12.275
not just like a personal habit,

01:25:12.275 --> 01:25:15.060
but was also required by law institutions,

01:25:15.060 --> 01:25:15.893
by military schools.

01:25:15.893 --> 01:25:17.680
So I'm just wondering when we work

01:25:17.680 --> 01:25:19.910
with this kinda sources as historians,

01:25:19.910 --> 01:25:21.621
how do acknowledge the background

01:25:21.621 --> 01:25:23.977
and some potential bias
that they might have,

01:25:23.977 --> 01:25:26.491
and how do you deal with
that in your research?

01:25:26.491 --> 01:25:27.573
Thank you so much.

01:25:27.573 --> 01:25:29.262
- [Arne] Could we do these two together?

01:25:29.262 --> 01:25:31.735
- Let's get them together, absolutely.

01:25:31.735 --> 01:25:33.417
- [Audience Member] Hi,
thank for your talk.

01:25:33.417 --> 01:25:36.121
Actually, my question is
similar to (indistinct).

01:25:36.121 --> 01:25:37.330
I was thinking about the use...

01:25:37.330 --> 01:25:40.230
Both the use of diaries in this talk,

01:25:40.230 --> 01:25:42.681
and also the juxtaposition of diaries.

01:25:42.681 --> 01:25:47.270
In some ways, all of them
are about calling into being

01:25:47.270 --> 01:25:48.370
a new kind of world,

01:25:48.370 --> 01:25:50.660
whether it's a kinda global order

01:25:50.660 --> 01:25:53.524
or a new kind self-identification.

01:25:53.524 --> 01:25:57.900
So I'd also like to ask you to speak more

01:25:57.900 --> 01:25:59.764
about the choice of the diaries,

01:25:59.764 --> 01:26:00.597
the use of the diaries,

01:26:00.597 --> 01:26:02.321
and then what is

01:26:02.321 --> 01:26:05.380
the audience for the diaries?

01:26:05.380 --> 01:26:08.290
And then, I guess the larger question, is

01:26:12.680 --> 01:26:13.513
how...

01:26:17.150 --> 01:26:19.650
(indistinct)

01:26:24.190 --> 01:26:26.728
How is the act of writing

01:26:26.728 --> 01:26:28.685
different from the act of speaking?

01:26:28.685 --> 01:26:33.181
And how is writing and being
this vision of the world

01:26:33.181 --> 01:26:36.264
different from speaking it and being?

01:26:40.630 --> 01:26:41.463
- Great, thanks.

01:26:41.463 --> 01:26:43.798
Two wonderful questions to finish off with

01:26:43.798 --> 01:26:45.263
in related areas.

01:26:45.263 --> 01:26:49.080
I mean, no diaries, or
no personal accounts,

01:26:49.080 --> 01:26:51.347
of any sort are, in any sense,

01:26:51.347 --> 01:26:53.670
pure, or unmediated, or authentic.

01:26:53.670 --> 01:26:56.540
Everything is constructed
in some particular idea.

01:26:56.540 --> 01:26:58.510
And one of the reasons I gave
a quick drive by shooting

01:26:58.510 --> 01:26:59.900
to the idea of the liberal subject,

01:26:59.900 --> 01:27:00.910
and hey, I'm a liberal subject,

01:27:00.910 --> 01:27:02.070
I love the liberal subject,

01:27:02.070 --> 01:27:03.447
is that we do...

01:27:03.447 --> 01:27:07.340
"We," university-educated,
Ivy League type people,

01:27:07.340 --> 01:27:09.390
living in the Western
world, speaking English,

01:27:09.390 --> 01:27:10.830
tend to assume it's normative

01:27:10.830 --> 01:27:12.570
and that other things are deviations

01:27:12.570 --> 01:27:13.840
or anomalies from them,

01:27:13.840 --> 01:27:16.330
e.g. the construction of the mind of

01:27:16.330 --> 01:27:19.130
a young communist cadre in the 1940s.

01:27:19.130 --> 01:27:20.980
So part of what I was trying
to do is point out that...

01:27:20.980 --> 01:27:22.450
Not that there's any sense in which

01:27:22.450 --> 01:27:23.960
these things are not constructed.

01:27:23.960 --> 01:27:27.050
I mean, part of the answer
to her question is that

01:27:27.050 --> 01:27:28.190
at that time,

01:27:28.190 --> 01:27:29.960
one of the ways in which you're expected

01:27:29.960 --> 01:27:32.850
to self-discipline is to
write personal accounts.

01:27:32.850 --> 01:27:34.160
And particularly, in the case of

01:27:34.160 --> 01:27:35.550
mid-level cadres and others,

01:27:35.550 --> 01:27:36.660
they would quite often be read,

01:27:36.660 --> 01:27:38.020
and inspected, and discussed

01:27:38.020 --> 01:27:40.460
by senior officers elsewhere as well.

01:27:40.460 --> 01:27:42.419
So the idea that this were purely

01:27:42.419 --> 01:27:44.520
private documents wouldn't
necessarily be the case.

01:27:44.520 --> 01:27:46.401
I would recommend, by the way,

01:27:46.401 --> 01:27:47.667
the fantastic book "Writing War,"

01:27:47.667 --> 01:27:48.500
or by Aaron Moore, Aaron William Moore,

01:27:48.500 --> 01:27:50.860
which is about different types of Chinese,

01:27:50.860 --> 01:27:53.780
Japanese, and indeed, American
service people's diaries,

01:27:53.780 --> 01:27:55.090
service men's diaries, mostly,

01:27:55.090 --> 01:27:59.030
and how they are used
to try and both mediate

01:27:59.030 --> 01:28:01.150
through a professional obligation

01:28:01.150 --> 01:28:05.000
and a sense of creation of the self.

01:28:05.000 --> 01:28:06.700
But I think one of the things...

01:28:06.700 --> 01:28:08.316
I mean, I think the answer to it...

01:28:08.316 --> 01:28:09.549
The other answer to your question is,

01:28:09.549 --> 01:28:11.091
how can we kind of use them productively?

01:28:11.091 --> 01:28:13.980
I think it's true as of
all these sorts of sources

01:28:13.980 --> 01:28:16.240
that you have to read against the grain,

01:28:16.240 --> 01:28:18.850
and there's the phrase that
has become the Stockholm,

01:28:18.850 --> 01:28:20.360
but it's useful in this sense.

01:28:20.360 --> 01:28:22.030
And in particular case,

01:28:22.030 --> 01:28:25.520
the things that I think
they're useful for are things

01:28:25.520 --> 01:28:27.260
like understanding where the flaws

01:28:27.260 --> 01:28:29.450
and difficulties in the
revolution come about,

01:28:29.450 --> 01:28:31.650
that are later smoothed out by the idea

01:28:31.650 --> 01:28:34.190
that actually everything
that party does goes well

01:28:34.190 --> 01:28:36.887
and that everyone follows
the revolutionary path.

01:28:36.887 --> 01:28:38.840
And one of the things that is clearest

01:28:38.840 --> 01:28:40.210
across these very different diaries

01:28:40.210 --> 01:28:42.400
from different people in
different parts of China

01:28:42.400 --> 01:28:44.500
at various times is that the revolution,

01:28:44.500 --> 01:28:45.333
surprise, surprise,

01:28:45.333 --> 01:28:47.610
was not a smooth and easy process

01:28:47.610 --> 01:28:50.160
and had a lot of complexities within it

01:28:50.160 --> 01:28:51.450
during that time.

01:28:51.450 --> 01:28:55.080
But the one thing I think
we probably can't do,

01:28:55.080 --> 01:28:57.900
and we can, I think, take pride in having

01:28:57.900 --> 01:29:01.810
to work around this is that
unlike Leopold von Ranke,

01:29:01.810 --> 01:29:02.987
the idea of (speaking foreign language),

01:29:04.057 --> 01:29:05.840
"how it really was,"

01:29:05.840 --> 01:29:08.190
that isn't really what
people's construction

01:29:08.190 --> 01:29:10.540
of themselves does

01:29:10.540 --> 01:29:12.420
because everything is going to be mediated

01:29:12.420 --> 01:29:14.290
through a set of positions,

01:29:14.290 --> 01:29:18.830
whether it's gender or class
or geography or emotion,

01:29:18.830 --> 01:29:20.340
which I think is one of
the really important ones.

01:29:20.340 --> 01:29:23.160
It is really different
reading diary entrances

01:29:23.160 --> 01:29:24.280
from Chen Bulei,

01:29:24.280 --> 01:29:26.620
who's a senior man who is suffering

01:29:26.620 --> 01:29:29.180
a mental health issue that
eventually will help him...

01:29:29.180 --> 01:29:31.125
Force him to take his own life,

01:29:31.125 --> 01:29:33.610
and the work, or the writings,

01:29:33.610 --> 01:29:36.200
of a 22 year old woman who is having

01:29:36.200 --> 01:29:40.630
a very, very bad personal
life experience at that time,

01:29:40.630 --> 01:29:42.810
in terms of her relationship,

01:29:42.810 --> 01:29:45.070
but on the other hand is
part of what she perceives,

01:29:45.070 --> 01:29:48.840
and repeatedly defines
herself as being part of,

01:29:48.840 --> 01:29:51.270
an ongoing transformative project

01:29:51.270 --> 01:29:53.510
of which she's very
enthusiastic to be a part.

01:29:53.510 --> 01:29:55.310
And all these things are visible

01:29:55.310 --> 01:29:57.430
in terms of the way in which
they present themselves,

01:29:57.430 --> 01:29:58.980
but they're not simply capable, I think,

01:29:58.980 --> 01:30:00.750
of being separated out as being

01:30:00.750 --> 01:30:02.670
either sort of positive or negative,

01:30:02.670 --> 01:30:04.900
or supportive or unsupportive,

01:30:04.900 --> 01:30:06.400
of a particular political project.

01:30:06.400 --> 01:30:08.200
That they're more complex than that.

01:30:09.480 --> 01:30:10.876
- Thank you very much, Rana.

01:30:10.876 --> 01:30:13.013
I was also thinking about
the gender dimensions,

01:30:13.013 --> 01:30:15.227
so they actually discussed it in

01:30:15.227 --> 01:30:16.940
my research seminar a couple of weeks ago.

01:30:16.940 --> 01:30:21.552
And the significance for
trying to understand theories

01:30:21.552 --> 01:30:23.970
of tremendous change,

01:30:23.970 --> 01:30:26.110
like the one that you are dealing with,

01:30:26.110 --> 01:30:30.555
it drew being able to
listen to women's voices

01:30:30.555 --> 01:30:32.837
from indifferent positions,

01:30:32.837 --> 01:30:34.451
and different situations.

01:30:34.451 --> 01:30:36.070
And often, even today,

01:30:36.070 --> 01:30:38.050
that is not done.

01:30:38.050 --> 01:30:39.775
I mean, it was already
things that struck me

01:30:39.775 --> 01:30:41.250
when I worked on the
civil war era in China,

01:30:41.250 --> 01:30:45.630
what a period of breakthroughs

01:30:45.630 --> 01:30:46.643
for women that was.

01:30:46.643 --> 01:30:49.090
I mean, in organizational terms,

01:30:49.090 --> 01:30:51.820
but also, in much broader terms.

01:30:51.820 --> 01:30:53.070
In intellectual life,

01:30:53.070 --> 01:30:55.604
in terms of what went
on in the countryside.

01:30:55.604 --> 01:30:58.160
So maybe we could have you back

01:30:58.160 --> 01:31:01.947
when this project is nearing completion

01:31:01.947 --> 01:31:03.120
(speaking over each other)

01:31:03.120 --> 01:31:05.560
- I mentioned another, another
dimension (indistinct).

01:31:05.560 --> 01:31:06.930
But first of all,

01:31:06.930 --> 01:31:09.373
thank you very much for a
wonderful lecture today.

01:31:10.223 --> 01:31:13.390
(audience applauding)

